/* Field study guides (owner, Nov 7 2026, for 1.2): one reference tab per field. */
DB.FGUIDE={"diet":{"tab":"Drug–Nutrient Interactions","sub":"How medicines and nutrition affect each other, for the RD exam","intro":"Medicines can change how nutrients are absorbed, used and lost, and food can change how medicines work. These tables cover the interactions dietitians see most.","sections":[{"h":"Absorption interference","p":"Some drugs block nutrient absorption by changing stomach acid or by binding nutrients in the gut.","cols":["Drug or class","Nutrient affected","Why it happens"],"rows":[["PPIs (omeprazole, pantoprazole)","Vitamin B12","Acid and pepsin free B12 from food protein"],["PPIs","Nonheme iron","Acid helps convert ferric to absorbable ferrous iron"],["PPIs","Calcium carbonate","Needs acid to dissolve; calcium citrate does not"],["PPIs (long term)","Magnesium","FDA warning for hypomagnesemia"],["Cholestyramine, colestipol","Vitamins A, D, E, K and folate","Resin binds bile acids and fat-soluble nutrients"],["Orlistat","Fat-soluble vitamins","Blocks fat digestion; multivitamin spaced apart in time"],["Metformin (long term)","Vitamin B12","Reduced ileal absorption; periodic B12 checks"],["Tetracycline, ciprofloxacin","Calcium, iron, magnesium, zinc","Minerals chelate the drug, lowering absorption of both"],["Isoniazid","Vitamin B6","Depletes pyridoxine; peripheral neuropathy risk"]],"tip":"A client on a PPI absorbs calcium citrate better than calcium carbonate."},{"h":"Excretion and depletion","p":"Diuretics change how the kidney handles electrolytes.","cols":["Drug or class","Electrolytes lost","Electrolytes retained"],"rows":[["Loop diuretics (furosemide)","Potassium, magnesium, calcium, sodium","None notable"],["Thiazides (hydrochlorothiazide)","Potassium, magnesium, sodium","Calcium (less urinary loss)"],["Spironolactone","Sodium","Potassium"],["Corticosteroids (prednisone)","Potassium, calcium","Sodium and water"],["Lithium","Sensitive to sodium and fluid shifts","Low sodium or dehydration raises lithium levels"]],"tip":"Loops lose calcium; thiazides keep calcium."},{"h":"Food and fasting timing","cols":["Drug","Timing with food","Reason"],"rows":[["Levothyroxine","Empty stomach, 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast","Food, coffee, fiber and soy lower absorption"],["Levothyroxine","About 4 hours apart from calcium or iron","Minerals bind it in the gut"],["Alendronate","First thing in the morning, plain water, upright 30 minutes","Food and drinks block absorption; esophagus irritation"],["Oral semaglutide","Empty stomach with a small sip of water, 30 minutes before food","Food lowers absorption"],["Metformin","With meals","Fewer GI effects"],["Phosphate binders","With meals or the first bite","Must meet dietary phosphorus in the gut"],["Carbidopa/levodopa","Watch high-protein meals","Amino acids compete for absorption and brain uptake"],["Statins (simvastatin, lovastatin)","Grapefruit is a concern","Grapefruit blocks CYP3A4, raising drug levels"]]},{"h":"MAOIs and tyramine","p":"MAOIs block breakdown of tyramine, which then releases norepinephrine and can cause a hypertensive crisis.","cols":["Food group","Higher tyramine","Lower tyramine"],"rows":[["Cheese","Aged cheddar, blue, Swiss, parmesan","Cottage, cream cheese, fresh mozzarella"],["Meat and fish","Salami, pepperoni, cured or smoked meats, pickled herring","Fresh meat, poultry and fish"],["Soy and ferments","Soy sauce, miso, sauerkraut, kimchi","Fresh soybeans in small amounts"],["Drinks","Tap or unpasteurized beer","Most bottled beer in moderation"],["Produce","Overripe or spoiled produce, fava bean pods","Fresh fruits and vegetables"],["Drugs with MAOI effect","Phenelzine, tranylcypromine, isocarboxazid","Linezolid has weaker MAOI activity"]],"tip":"Warning signs: severe headache, pounding heart, stiff neck, very high blood pressure. Restrictions continue about 2 weeks after an MAOI ends."},{"h":"Side effects that affect intake","p":"Some drugs change weight, glucose, GI comfort or appetite; others are used to boost appetite.","cols":["Drug or class","Effect","What the RD watches for"],"rows":[["Olanzapine, clozapine","Most weight gain of antipsychotics; hyperglycemia; high lipids","Weight, waist, fasting glucose or A1C, lipids"],["Clozapine","Severe constipation, drooling","Fiber, fluids, bowel habits"],["Prednisone","Hyperglycemia (afternoon/evening), fluid retention, appetite gain","Glucose, sodium, bone health, muscle loss"],["Metformin","Nausea, diarrhea, metallic taste","Usually eases with food and time"],["Chemotherapy","Nausea, mucositis, dysgeusia, neutropenia","Soft bland foods, plastic utensils, food safety"],["Antibiotics (clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins)","C. diff diarrhea","Frequent watery stools, fever, dehydration"],["Stimulants (methylphenidate, amphetamines)","Appetite suppression in children","Growth, weight; big breakfast and evening snack"],["Mirtazapine","Increased appetite and weight gain; sometimes used off-label for this","Weight trend, sedation"],["Megestrol (appetite stimulant)","Appetite and weight gain in AIDS cachexia","Gain mostly fat and fluid; blood clots, adrenal suppression"],["Dronabinol (appetite stimulant)","AIDS anorexia; chemotherapy nausea","Drowsiness, confusion, mood changes"]]},{"h":"Insulin action profiles","p":"Hypoglycemia risk is highest at the peak.","cols":["Insulin","Onset / peak / duration","Highest hypoglycemia risk"],"rows":[["Rapid: lispro, aspart, glulisine","About 15 min / 1-2 h / 3-5 h","1-2 hours after the meal dose"],["Short: regular","30-60 min / 2-4 h / 5-8 h","2-4 hours later; given about 30 minutes before meals"],["Intermediate: NPH","1-2 h / 4-12 h / 12-18 h","Morning dose: afternoon; evening dose: overnight"],["Long: glargine","1-2 h / no clear peak / about 24 h","Low; steady basal level"],["Long: detemir (US brand ended 2024)","1-2 h / relatively flat / up to 24 h","Low; steady basal level"],["Ultra-long: degludec","About 1 h / no peak / over 42 h","Low; steady basal level"]],"tip":"Breakfast NPH peaks in the afternoon; a missed lunch is the classic setup for a low."},{"h":"Other diabetes medicines","cols":["Drug or class","How it works","Nutrition points"],"rows":[["Metformin","Lowers liver glucose output; improves insulin sensitivity","GI upset; B12 deficiency; low hypoglycemia risk alone"],["SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin)","Kidney excretes glucose in urine","Hydration; genital yeast and UTIs; modest weight loss"],["SGLT2 inhibitors","Ketone risk","Euglycemic DKA with keto diets, fasting, illness"],["GLP-1 agonists (semaglutide)","More glucose-dependent insulin, less glucagon, slower emptying","Nausea, vomiting, constipation; early fullness"],["GLP-1 agonists","Weight loss","Adequate protein and strength training to protect lean mass"],["GLP-1 agonists","Fluid losses from GI effects","Dehydration and kidney injury; gallbladder and pancreatitis warnings"],["Sulfonylureas (glipizide)","More insulin release","Hypoglycemia with missed meals"]]},{"h":"Critical care and tube feeding","p":"ICU drugs can add calories or interact with enteral formula.","cols":["Drug or issue","Interaction","What the RD does"],"rows":[["Propofol","10% lipid emulsion, about 1.1 kcal/mL","Count as fat calories; adjust regimen"],["Propofol (prolonged)","Hypertriglyceridemia, overfeeding","Monitor triglycerides; watch egg and soy allergy"],["Clevidipine","20% lipid emulsion, about 2 kcal/mL","Count as fat calories"],["Phenytoin","Binds formula; absorption falls","Feeds separated around the dose; levels monitored"],["Ciprofloxacin, tetracycline","Chelate formula minerals","Separate timing from feeds"],["Warfarin","Formula vitamin K and protein binding","INR checks when feeds start or change"],["Sucralfate, psyllium, bulk agents","Clump with formula; clog tubes","Flag for pharmacist review"],["Liquid medicines","Sorbitol causes diarrhea","Review medication list before blaming formula"],["Tube care","Clogs from crushed meds","Water flush before and after each drug; no crushing extended-release"]],"tip":"Propofol is fat: about 1.1 kcal/mL counts toward the lipid calories."},{"h":"Renal and cardiovascular","cols":["Drug","Nutrition link","What to watch"],"rows":[["Sevelamer","Phosphate binder, no calcium; with meals","Phosphorus; may lower LDL; GI effects"],["Calcium acetate","Phosphate binder with the first bite","Hypercalcemia"],["Lanthanum carbonate","Chewable binder with or right after meals","Chewed completely; GI effects"],["Warfarin","Vitamin K reverses its effect","Consistent vitamin K; more greens lower INR"],["ACE inhibitors (lisinopril), ARBs (losartan)","Less aldosterone, potassium retained","Hyperkalemia; salt substitutes with potassium chloride"],["Spironolactone","Potassium-sparing","Additive hyperkalemia with ACE inhibitors or ARBs"],["Potassium-restricted diet","Limit bananas, potatoes, oranges, tomatoes","Leaching potatoes lowers potassium"]],"tip":"Warfarin: consistency, not avoidance, of vitamin K."}]},"rrt":{"tab":"Inhaled & Critical Care Meds","sub":"Aerosols, RSI, ICU sedation, inhaled vasodilators and ACLS drugs for RTs","intro":"The medicines a respiratory therapist delivers, monitors or sees at the bedside, organized the way the NBRC TMC and CSE exams test them. Focus on mechanism, expected effects and what to watch for and report.","sections":[{"h":"Bronchodilators: beta agonists and antimuscarinics","p":"Beta-2 agonists relax airway smooth muscle; antimuscarinics block vagal bronchoconstriction. Short-acting agents are rescue; long-acting agents are maintenance.","cols":["Drug / class","Key points","Watch for / report"],"rows":[["Albuterol (SABA)","Rescue bronchodilator; onset within minutes, lasts about 4-6 hours","Tachycardia, palpitations, tremor, low potassium"],["Levalbuterol (SABA)","Pure R-isomer of albuterol; often chosen for cardiac patients","May cause less tachycardia; still monitor heart rate"],["Terbutaline (SABA)","Subcutaneous option in severe asthma when aerosol is not reaching airways","Tachycardia, tremor, hyperglycemia"],["Salmeterol, formoterol (LABA)","Maintenance; salmeterol has slow onset and is never a rescue drug","In asthma, LABA is paired with an ICS, never used alone"],["Arformoterol, olodaterol, indacaterol (LABA)","Twice- or once-daily COPD maintenance","Same beta-agonist effects; not for acute relief"],["Ipratropium (SAMA)","Blocks muscarinic receptors; onset slower than albuterol","Dry mouth; mask leak into eyes can dilate pupils and raise eye pressure"],["Tiotropium, umeclidinium (LAMA)","Once-daily COPD maintenance","Dry mouth, urinary retention, narrow-angle glaucoma"],["Albuterol-ipratropium (DuoNeb)","Two mechanisms give more bronchodilation than either alone","Combined beta-agonist and anticholinergic effects"],["Racemic epinephrine","Alpha effect shrinks swollen upper airway mucosa (croup, post-extubation stridor)","Rebound swelling; observe for several hours after"]],"tip":"Breath sounds, peak flow and work of breathing before and after a treatment show whether a bronchodilator worked; heart rate shows what it cost."},{"h":"Inhaled corticosteroids and combinations","p":"ICS reduce airway inflammation over days to weeks. They do not relieve acute bronchospasm.","cols":["Drug","Delivery / role","Watch for / teach"],"rows":[["Fluticasone","MDI or DPI; daily controller for asthma","Rinse mouth and spit after use to prevent thrush"],["Budesonide","Only ICS sold as a nebulizer suspension; used in young children","Thrush, hoarseness; wipe face after mask treatments"],["Beclomethasone, mometasone, ciclesonide","MDI or DPI controllers","Oral candidiasis, dysphonia; spacer reduces throat deposition"],["Fluticasone-salmeterol, budesonide-formoterol","ICS plus LABA maintenance","Budesonide-formoterol may also serve as reliever in some asthma plans"],["Fluticasone-umeclidinium-vilanterol","Once-daily triple therapy for COPD","Thrush, pneumonia risk with ICS in COPD"],["Albuterol-budesonide","Rescue inhaler that adds an ICS to each use","Same SABA effects plus ICS local effects"]],"tip":"Hoarseness and white oral patches in an ICS user point to technique and mouth-rinsing teaching first."},{"h":"Mucolytics and airway clearance","p":"These thin or loosen secretions so airway clearance can remove them. Many can trigger bronchospasm, so a bronchodilator is given first.","cols":["Agent","How it works","RT considerations"],"rows":[["Acetylcysteine (inhaled)","Breaks disulfide bonds in mucus","Can cause bronchospasm; given with or after a bronchodilator; rotten-egg odor"],["Hypertonic saline (inhaled)","Draws water into airway surface liquid","Cough and bronchospasm; bronchodilator pretreatment"],["Dornase alfa","Enzyme that cleaves neutrophil DNA in CF sputum","Not mixed with other drugs in the nebulizer; voice change, sore throat"],["Mannitol (inhaled)","Osmotic agent for CF","Requires a tolerance test for bronchospasm"],["Tobramycin inhaled, aztreonam inhaled","Antibiotics for Pseudomonas in CF","Given after airway clearance so drug reaches cleaner airways"],["Typical CF sequence","Bronchodilator, hypertonic saline, dornase, airway clearance, inhaled antibiotic, ICS","Order maximizes deposition where it matters"]],"tip":"Any mucolytic that can irritate the airway: bronchodilator first, then reassess breath sounds."},{"h":"Rapid sequence intubation: neuromuscular blockers","p":"Paralytics give no sedation or pain relief. An induction agent always comes first.","cols":["Drug","Key points","Watch for / risks"],"rows":[["Succinylcholine (depolarizing)","Fastest onset, shortest duration; fasciculations then paralysis","Hyperkalemia, malignant hyperthermia, bradycardia in children"],["Succinylcholine: high-risk patients","Burns, crush or major trauma after the first day; denervation, neuromuscular disease, prolonged immobility","Upregulated receptors release large amounts of potassium"],["Succinylcholine: prolonged block","Plasma pseudocholinesterase deficiency","Paralysis lasting hours; needs full ventilation and sedation"],["Rocuronium (non-depolarizing)","Fast onset suitable for RSI; no fasciculations","Longer duration; reversed by sugammadex"],["Vecuronium (non-depolarizing)","Slower onset than rocuronium; must be reconstituted","Prolonged effect in liver or kidney failure"],["Sugammadex","Encapsulates rocuronium or vecuronium","Does not reverse succinylcholine or cisatracurium"]],"tip":"Rising ETCO2, jaw rigidity and fever after succinylcholine = malignant hyperthermia; dantrolene is the antidote."},{"h":"Rapid sequence intubation: induction agents","cols":["Agent","Strength","Watch for"],"rows":[["Propofol","Rapid onset and offset","Hypotension from vasodilation and myocardial depression; apnea"],["Etomidate","Little change in blood pressure or heart rate","Transient adrenal suppression, myoclonus, nausea"],["Ketamine","Bronchodilates; preserves drive and airway reflexes","Increased secretions, emergence reactions, raised heart rate and BP"],["Midazolam","Amnesia; sometimes used for induction","Hypotension and slower onset than other agents"]],"tip":"Status asthmaticus needing intubation: ketamine is the classic induction choice."},{"h":"ICU sedation, analgesia and ventilator liberation","p":"Current guidelines favor analgesia first, light sedation, and daily paired awakening and breathing trials.","cols":["Drug or practice","Key points","Watch for / report"],"rows":[["Dexmedetomidine","Alpha-2 agonist; arousable sedation with little respiratory depression","Bradycardia, hypotension"],["Propofol infusion","Titratable, quick offset for daily awakening","Hypotension, high triglycerides, propofol infusion syndrome"],["Fentanyl","Opioid analgesia; lowers respiratory rate and drive","Apnea on spontaneous modes, rising PaCO2, chest wall rigidity with rapid push"],["Midazolam","Benzodiazepine; accumulates with prolonged infusion","Delayed awakening, delirium, longer ventilation"],["Spontaneous awakening trial (SAT)","Sedatives interrupted by the care team to assess wakefulness","Agitation, distress, arrhythmia, falling SpO2"],["Spontaneous breathing trial (SBT)","Low pressure support, CPAP or T-piece after a passed SAT","RSBI under about 105 favors success; report distress"],["Cisatracurium","Non-depolarizing; Hofmann elimination independent of liver and kidney","Used early in severe ARDS for synchrony"],["Paralysis safety","Deep sedation and analgesia confirmed before and during paralysis","Awareness, corneal injury, ICU-acquired weakness"],["Train-of-four monitoring","Peripheral nerve stimulator guides depth of blockade","Report absent twitches or over-recovery"]],"tip":"A paralyzed patient cannot breathe: full support mode, set rate, tight alarms and a bag-valve mask at the bedside."},{"h":"Pulmonary hypertension and inhaled vasodilators","p":"Inhaled vasodilators reach only ventilated alveoli, improving V/Q matching with little systemic effect.","cols":["Agent","Use and action","Monitor / risks"],"rows":[["Inhaled nitric oxide","Raises cGMP; FDA-approved for term and near-term neonates with hypoxic respiratory failure and PPHN","NO2, methemoglobin, rebound on abrupt withdrawal"],["iNO in ARDS","Off-label rescue; improves oxygenation","No proven mortality benefit"],["iNO delivery","Injected into inspiratory limb, sampled near patient wye","Continuous NO and NO2 analysis; backup delivery for bagging"],["iNO cautions","Severe left ventricular dysfunction; duct-dependent heart lesions","Pulmonary edema from increased pulmonary blood flow"],["Epoprostenol (continuous nebulization)","Prostacyclin; half-life of minutes","Rebound pulmonary hypertension if delivery interrupted"],["Epoprostenol on the ventilator","Drug deposits on expiratory filter","Rising pressures and auto-PEEP from clogged filter; scheduled filter changes"],["Iloprost, inhaled treprostinil","Intermittent inhaled prostacyclins for PAH","Cough, flushing, headache, jaw pain"],["Sildenafil, tadalafil","Oral PDE-5 inhibitors for PAH","May blunt rebound during iNO weaning; hypotension with nitrates"]],"tip":"Sudden hypoxemia and hypotension on iNO or nebulized epoprostenol: check first whether delivery was interrupted."},{"h":"ACLS and reversal agents","cols":["Drug","Key points","Watch for"],"rows":[["Epinephrine","Vasoconstriction raises coronary perfusion pressure; every 3-5 minutes in arrest","Given early in asystole/PEA; after shocks in VF/pVT"],["Amiodarone","First antiarrhythmic for shock-refractory VF or pulseless VT","Hypotension, bradycardia; long-term pulmonary toxicity"],["Lidocaine","Alternative to amiodarone in refractory VF/pVT","Confusion, seizures with toxicity"],["Sodium bicarbonate","Not routine; hyperkalemia, tricyclic overdose","Generates CO2; ETCO2 and PaCO2 rise"],["Endotracheal route","Naloxone, atropine, epinephrine, lidocaine can go via ETT","IV or IO is preferred"],["Naloxone","Reverses opioids; IV onset about 1-2 minutes","Re-sedation as it wears off; withdrawal; pulmonary edema"],["Flumazenil","Reverses benzodiazepines; onset about 1-2 minutes","Re-sedation; seizures in chronic benzodiazepine users"]],"tip":"Reversal agents often wear off before the drug they reverse: keep monitoring respiratory rate, depth, SpO2 and ETCO2."}]},"mh":{"tab":"Psych Meds Essentials","sub":"Antidepressants, mood stabilizers, antipsychotics, anxiolytics, addiction and ADHD medicines","intro":"Core psychiatric medicines that mental and behavioral health professionals see every day. Focus on what to teach, what to watch for and what to report right away.","sections":[{"h":"SSRIs and SNRIs","p":"First-line antidepressants for depression and anxiety disorders. Benefit builds slowly while side effects often appear first.","cols":["Medicine","Class","Key points"],"rows":[["Fluoxetine","SSRI","Very long half-life; activating for some; long washout before an MAOI"],["Sertraline","SSRI","Common first choice; early GI upset and loose stools"],["Escitalopram","SSRI","Well tolerated; QT effects less than citalopram"],["Paroxetine","SSRI","Sedating, anticholinergic; strong discontinuation symptoms if missed"],["Duloxetine","SNRI","Also for neuropathic pain and fibromyalgia; report jaundice or dark urine"],["Venlafaxine","SNRI","Can raise blood pressure; marked discontinuation symptoms"],["Onset","All","Some early gains in 1-2 weeks; full effect usually 4-6 weeks"],["Boxed warning","All antidepressants","More suicidal thoughts in children, teens, young adults early in treatment or after dose changes"],["Common effects","All","Nausea, headache, insomnia or drowsiness, sexual dysfunction"]],"tip":"Energy can return before mood lifts: watch closely for suicide risk in the first weeks."},{"h":"Lithium and Other Mood Stabilizers","p":"Used for bipolar disorder. Lithium has a narrow therapeutic range, so levels and early toxicity signs matter.","cols":["Topic","What to know"],"rows":[["Lithium levels","Trough drawn about 12 hours after the last dose; more often early and after changes"],["Other lithium labs","Kidney function, thyroid function, sodium; ECG in older adults"],["Expected effects","Fine hand tremor, thirst, frequent urination, mild nausea, weight gain"],["Toxicity signs","Coarse tremor, vomiting, diarrhea, slurred speech, unsteady gait, confusion: report at once"],["Raises lithium","Dehydration, low sodium intake, heavy sweating, NSAIDs, thiazides, ACE inhibitors"],["Teaching","Keep fluid and salt intake steady; report illness with vomiting or diarrhea"],["Valproate","Boxed warnings: liver injury, pancreatitis, birth defects (neural tube); watch LFTs, platelets, levels"],["Lamotrigine","Serious rash risk (Stevens-Johnson); report any new rash"],["Carbamazepine","Serious rash (HLA-B*1502), low blood counts, low sodium, many drug interactions"]],"tip":"Fine tremor is expected; coarse tremor with confusion or slurred speech suggests toxicity."},{"h":"MAOIs and Serotonin Syndrome","p":"MAOIs such as phenelzine work well but need diet and drug-interaction precautions.","cols":["Issue","Details"],"rows":[["Examples","Phenelzine, tranylcypromine, isocarboxazid, selegiline patch"],["Tyramine foods","Aged cheese, cured or fermented meats, tap beer, soy sauce, sauerkraut, spoiled leftovers"],["Hypertensive crisis","Sudden severe occipital headache, stiff neck, palpitations, sweating, very high BP"],["Interacting drugs","SSRIs, SNRIs, TCAs, meperidine, tramadol, dextromethorphan, linezolid, stimulants, decongestants"],["Washout","About 2 weeks between an MAOI and other serotonergic drugs; 5 weeks after fluoxetine"],["Serotonin syndrome","Agitation, sweating, fever, tremor, hyperreflexia, clonus, diarrhea; rapid onset"],["Diet duration","Precautions continue about 2 weeks after the last MAOI dose"]],"tip":"Clonus and hyperreflexia point to serotonin syndrome; lead-pipe rigidity points to NMS."},{"h":"First-Generation Antipsychotics and EPS","p":"Typical antipsychotics block dopamine strongly. High-potency agents carry the most movement side effects.","cols":["Effect","Timing","What it looks like"],"rows":[["Acute dystonia","Hours to days","Neck twisting, eyes rolled up, jaw or tongue spasm; treated with benztropine or diphenhydramine"],["Akathisia","Days to weeks","Inner restlessness, pacing, cannot sit still; often mistaken for anxiety"],["Parkinsonism","Weeks","Shuffling gait, masklike face, rigidity, slow movement, pill-rolling tremor"],["Tardive dyskinesia","Months to years","Lip smacking, tongue thrusting, grimacing; may be permanent"],["Screening","Baseline and regularly","AIMS exam for involuntary movements"],["High potency","Haloperidol, fluphenazine","Most EPS, less sedation"],["Low potency","Chlorpromazine, thioridazine","More sedation, anticholinergic effects, orthostasis"],["Long-acting injection","Haloperidol or fluphenazine decanoate","Supports adherence; effects last weeks"]],"tip":"Acute dystonia affecting the throat can threaten the airway: report immediately."},{"h":"Second-Generation Antipsychotics","p":"Atypical antipsychotics cause fewer EPS but more metabolic problems.","cols":["Medicine","Notable risks"],"rows":[["Olanzapine","Major weight gain, high glucose and lipids, sedation"],["Quetiapine","Sedation, orthostatic hypotension, weight gain"],["Risperidone","High prolactin (breast changes, missed periods), dose-related EPS"],["Aripiprazole","Akathisia; less weight gain; impulse-control problems"],["Ziprasidone","QT prolongation; absorbed better with food"],["Clozapine","Severe neutropenia (ANC monitoring), seizures, myocarditis, constipation; for treatment-resistant illness"],["Metabolic monitoring","Weight/BMI, waist, BP, fasting glucose or A1c, lipids at baseline and regularly"],["Boxed warning (all)","Higher death rate in older adults with dementia-related psychosis"]],"tip":"Metabolic syndrome is the big SGA risk; clozapine's big risk is low neutrophils."},{"h":"Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome","p":"A rare, life-threatening reaction to dopamine blockers. Treat it as an emergency.","cols":["Feature","NMS","Serotonin syndrome"],"rows":[["Cause","Dopamine blockers (antipsychotics)","Serotonergic drugs, especially combinations"],["Onset","Over days","Within hours"],["Muscles","Lead-pipe rigidity","Clonus, hyperreflexia, tremor"],["Temperature","High fever","Fever, sweating"],["Mental status","Confusion, stupor","Agitation, restlessness"],["Autonomic","Unstable BP, fast heart rate, sweating","Fast heart rate, high BP, diarrhea"],["Key lab","Very high CK, high WBC","No specific lab"],["Response","Emergency: report immediately, cooling and supportive care","Emergency: report immediately"]],"tip":"Fever plus rigidity plus confusion in someone on an antipsychotic = think NMS."},{"h":"Benzodiazepines and Buspirone","p":"Benzodiazepines act fast but cause dependence. Buspirone is slow but not habit forming.","cols":["Medicine","Features","Watch for"],"rows":[["Alprazolam","Short-acting, fast onset","Rebound anxiety between doses; high misuse potential"],["Lorazepam","Intermediate; no active metabolites","Used in alcohol withdrawal and agitation; sedation"],["Diazepam","Long-acting, active metabolites","Accumulates in older adults: falls, confusion"],["Clonazepam","Long-acting","Panic disorder, seizures; dependence"],["With alcohol or opioids","Boxed warning","Deep sedation, slowed breathing, coma, death"],["Withdrawal","After regular use","Anxiety, insomnia, tremor, seizures; changes are tapered by prescriber"],["Overdose reversal","Flumazenil","Can trigger seizures in dependent patients"],["Buspirone","Non-benzodiazepine anxiolytic","Not habit forming; taken daily, takes 2-4 weeks; not for as-needed use"]],"tip":"Benzodiazepine and alcohol withdrawal can both cause seizures; opioid withdrawal usually does not."},{"h":"Medicines for Substance Use Disorders","cols":["Medicine","How it works","Key teaching"],"rows":[["Methadone","Full opioid agonist","Opioid use disorder via certified treatment programs; QT prolongation, sedation"],["Buprenorphine","Partial opioid agonist","Started in mild-moderate withdrawal to avoid precipitated withdrawal; ceiling on breathing effects"],["Buprenorphine-naloxone","Partial agonist plus antagonist","Naloxone discourages injecting; dissolves under the tongue"],["Naltrexone","Opioid antagonist","For alcohol and opioid use disorder; needs 7-10 opioid-free days first"],["Naltrexone (after use)","Tolerance falls","Higher overdose risk if opioids are used later"],["Disulfiram","Blocks alcohol breakdown","Flushing, vomiting, pounding headache, low BP with any alcohol"],["Hidden alcohol","Disulfiram teaching","Mouthwash, cough syrups, some sauces, vinegars, skin products"],["Acamprosate","Restores glutamate balance","Reduces alcohol cravings; kidney function matters"],["Naloxone","Opioid reversal","Short-acting; overdose can return as it wears off"]],"tip":"Disulfiram reactions can occur up to about 2 weeks after the last dose."},{"h":"ADHD Medicines","p":"Stimulants work within an hour; non-stimulants take weeks.","cols":["Medicine","Type","Watch for"],"rows":[["Methylphenidate","Stimulant, Schedule II","Insomnia, low appetite, headache, stomach ache"],["Amphetamine salts","Stimulant, Schedule II","Boxed warning: misuse, addiction; raised HR and BP"],["Lisdexamfetamine","Prodrug stimulant","Activated in the blood; still Schedule II"],["Growth","Children on stimulants","Track height and weight on growth charts"],["Heart","All stimulants","Ask about heart disease, fainting, family sudden death"],["Circulation","All stimulants","Fingers or toes turning white, blue or painful (Raynaud's)"],["Psychiatric","All stimulants","New psychosis, mania, worsening tics or aggression: report"],["Diversion","All stimulants","Store securely; sharing or selling is illegal and dangerous"],["Atomoxetine","Non-stimulant (NE reuptake)","Not controlled; weeks for full effect; boxed warning for suicidal thoughts; liver injury"],["Viloxazine","Non-stimulant","Boxed warning for suicidal thoughts; drowsiness"],["Clonidine ER, guanfacine ER","Alpha-2 agonists","Drowsiness, low BP; rebound high BP if ended abruptly"]],"tip":"Atomoxetine is the go-to answer for ADHD with a substance misuse history."}]},"pt":{"tab":"Meds & Rehab","sub":"How common medicines change what you see in PT and OT sessions","intro":"Many patients in rehab take medicines that change alertness, strength, blood pressure, heart rate and healing. Knowing these effects helps you time sessions, read vital signs correctly, and spot problems to report.","sections":[{"h":"Muscle relaxants and antispasticity drugs","p":"Spasticity drugs reduce tone but can also reduce the strength patients use to stand and transfer.","cols":["Drug","How it works","What you may see in therapy"],"rows":[["Baclofen (oral)","GABA-B agonist in the spinal cord","Drowsiness, weakness, dizziness; less extensor tone to help standing"],["Baclofen (intrathecal pump)","Delivered to spinal fluid by implanted pump","Big tone drop with less sedation; watch pump alarms and refill dates"],["Tizanidine","Central alpha-2 agonist","Sedation, low blood pressure, dry mouth; liver tests monitored"],["Dantrolene","Acts in muscle; blocks calcium release","Generalized weakness; liver toxicity risk; also used for malignant hyperthermia"],["Diazepam","Benzodiazepine; boosts GABA-A","Sedation, poor balance, falls in older adults"],["Botulinum toxin (OnabotulinumtoxinA)","Blocks acetylcholine release at injected muscle","Focal weakness starts in days, peaks in weeks, wears off in about 3 months; pair with stretching and splinting"],["Cyclobenzaprine","Central; structurally like tricyclic antidepressants","Drowsiness, dry mouth, confusion; for acute muscle spasm, not spasticity"],["Methocarbamol, metaxalone, carisoprodol","Central sedating spasm relievers","Sedation and dizziness; carisoprodol has misuse risk"]],"tip":"Abrupt loss of baclofen (especially a pump failure) can cause high fever, rebound spasticity, confusion and seizures: an emergency."},{"h":"Pain medicines and session timing","p":"Good pain control lets patients do more, but some effects change safety during exercise.","cols":["Drug group","Timing pearl","Therapy concern"],"rows":[["Oral opioids (oxycodone, hydrocodone, morphine)","Peak about 1 hour after an oral immediate-release dose","Sedation, orthostasis, slowed breathing, constipation"],["Extended-release opioids and fentanyl patch","Steady level; not for quick relief","Heat on a fentanyl patch raises absorption: avoid heat modalities over it"],["Tramadol and tapentadol","Peak in about 1 to 2 hours","Dizziness; tramadol lowers seizure threshold and adds serotonin risk"],["NSAIDs (ibuprofen, naproxen, meloxicam, celecoxib)","Pain relief within about 1 hour","GI bleeding, kidney strain, fluid retention, raised blood pressure"],["Acetaminophen","Pain relief within about 1 hour","No anti-inflammatory effect; liver harm with excess use from combination products"],["Topical diclofenac, lidocaine patch","Local effect","Little sedation; useful before activity"],["Gabapentin and pregabalin","Used for nerve pain","Dizziness, sleepiness, swelling of the legs, unsteady gait"]],"tip":"Pain relief can mask warning pain, so keep exercise within protocol limits even when the patient feels fine."},{"h":"Corticosteroids and bone health","p":"Steroids calm inflammation but slow repair of many tissues.","cols":["Effect","Why it matters in rehab","What to watch for"],"rows":[["Slowed collagen and wound healing","Tendons, ligaments and incisions are weaker longer","Go gently with loading after repeated injections"],["Bone loss (osteoporosis)","Fracture risk with falls or forceful mobilization","Vertebral compression fracture signs; history of long-term use"],["Steroid myopathy","Proximal weakness, mostly hips and shoulders","Trouble rising from a chair or climbing stairs"],["High blood glucose","Can affect energy and wound healing","Thirst, frequent urination, fatigue"],["Skin thinning and easy bruising","Skin tears with transfers and tape","Careful handling and padding"],["Local injection effects","Tendon weakening after repeated injections","Pain flare for a day or two after injection"],["Infection risk and masked signs","Fever and redness may be muted","Report new warmth, drainage or malaise"]],"tip":"Alendronate is taken upright on an empty stomach with water; patients stay upright for at least 30 minutes afterward, which can affect early-morning positioning."},{"h":"Parkinson's medicines","p":"Motor control rises and falls with levodopa levels, so session timing matters.","cols":["Drug","Role","Therapy pearl"],"rows":[["Carbidopa/levodopa","Most effective motor drug; levodopa becomes dopamine","Schedule therapy during peak 'on' time, often about 1 hour after a dose"],["Entacapone, opicapone, tolcapone (COMT inhibitors)","Extend each levodopa dose","Less wearing off; may increase dyskinesia"],["Pramipexole, ropinirole, rotigotine (dopamine agonists)","Stimulate dopamine receptors directly","Sudden sleep, orthostasis, impulse-control problems, hallucinations"],["Rasagiline, selegiline, safinamide (MAO-B inhibitors)","Slow dopamine breakdown","Mild boost; interaction concerns with some opioids and antidepressants"],["Amantadine","Reduces levodopa-induced dyskinesia","Leg swelling, mottled skin (livedo reticularis), confusion"],["Apomorphine","Fast rescue for sudden 'off' episodes","Works within minutes; nausea and low blood pressure"],["Istradefylline","Add-on that reduces 'off' time","May worsen dyskinesia"],["Trihexyphenidyl","Anticholinergic for tremor","Confusion, dry mouth, constipation; poorly tolerated by older adults"]],"tip":"A high-protein meal near a levodopa dose can blunt absorption, because protein competes for gut and brain transport."},{"h":"On/off states and motor fluctuations","cols":["Term","What it looks like","Therapy response"],"rows":[["On","Good mobility after a dose works","Best time for gait, balance and skill training"],["Off","Slowness, rigidity, freezing, tremor return","Focus on safety, cueing and stretching; document timing"],["Wearing off","Symptoms return before the next dose is due","Note the time and report the pattern"],["Peak-dose dyskinesia","Writhing, dance-like extra movements at highest level","Expect more movement; guard for balance"],["Freezing of gait","Feet feel stuck, often at doorways or turns","Visual or auditory cues, weight shifting"],["Delayed or no 'on'","Dose fails to kick in, often after a protein meal","Report; ask about meal timing"]]},{"h":"Blood pressure and heart rate drugs","p":"These drugs change the vital-sign response you rely on to dose exercise.","cols":["Drug group","Exercise effect","What to do in therapy"],"rows":[["Beta-blockers (metoprolol, atenolol, propranolol)","Blunted resting and exercise heart rate","Use RPE or talk test instead of target heart rate"],["ACE inhibitors (lisinopril)","Lower blood pressure; dry cough","Check orthostatic vitals with position changes"],["Calcium channel blockers (amlodipine)","Vasodilation; ankle swelling","Longer cool-down to avoid post-exercise hypotension"],["Diuretics (furosemide, hydrochlorothiazide)","Fluid loss and potassium shifts","Dehydration, cramps, dizziness, irregular heartbeat"],["Tamsulosin (alpha-blocker)","Low blood pressure on standing","First-dose and position-change dizziness"],["Vasodilating effect of warm water and heat","Adds to drug-induced hypotension","Monitor after aquatic therapy or hot packs"]],"tip":"Orthostatic hypotension: a drop of at least 20 systolic or 10 diastolic within 3 minutes of standing."},{"h":"Diabetes medicines and exercise","cols":["Drug","Exercise risk","What to watch for"],"rows":[["Insulin lispro (rapid-acting)","Hypoglycemia, peaking about 1 to 2 hours after injection","Shaking, sweating, confusion; have fast sugar available"],["Insulin glargine (long-acting)","Lower but steady hypoglycemia risk","Late lows after long or hard sessions"],["Injection site near working muscle","Faster insulin absorption during exercise","Leg injection before leg exercise raises low risk"],["Metformin","Low hypoglycemia risk alone","GI upset; rare lactic acidosis"],["Beta-blockers with insulin","Can mask a racing heart from low glucose","Sweating may be the main warning sign"],["Pre-exercise glucose check","Low or very high values make exercise unsafe","Follow facility protocol and report"]],"tip":"Hypoglycemia can appear hours after exercise, even overnight."},{"h":"Anticoagulants, sedatives and fall risk","p":"Some drugs make falls more likely, and others make falls more dangerous.","cols":["Drug","Risk","What to watch for"],"rows":[["Warfarin","Bleeding; INR guides control","Bruising, joint swelling, blood in urine or stool"],["Apixaban, rivaroxaban","Bleeding with no routine lab check","Head strike after a fall needs urgent evaluation"],["Enoxaparin","Injected anticoagulant","Bruising at injection sites; avoid deep pressure there"],["Zolpidem, diazepam","Sedation, poor balance, next-morning drowsiness","Night-time and early morning falls"],["Diphenhydramine","Anticholinergic confusion in older adults","Beers Criteria drug to report"],["Opioids plus sedatives","Combined sedation and slowed breathing","Excess sleepiness, slow shallow breaths"],["Trazodone","Sedation and orthostasis","Dizziness on rising at night"],["Oxybutynin","Anticholinergic confusion","Cognitive change in older adults"]],"tip":"After any fall in a patient on an anticoagulant, report it promptly even if they look fine: bleeding inside the head can develop slowly."}]},"slp":{"tab":"Swallowing & Speech Meds","sub":"How medicines affect saliva, swallowing, alertness, voice and communication","intro":"Many medicines change saliva, muscle control, alertness, cognition and voice. Knowing these effects helps SLPs interpret evaluation findings, time sessions well and know what to report to the medical team.","sections":[{"h":"Xerostomia and Anticholinergic Burden","p":"Medicines that block acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors reduce saliva. Burden adds up when a patient takes several of them.","cols":["Drug class","Examples","SLP relevance"],"rows":[["First-generation antihistamines","Diphenhydramine, hydroxyzine","Dry mouth plus sedation and confusion in older adults"],["Tricyclic antidepressants","Amitriptyline, nortriptyline, doxepin","Strong dry mouth; amitriptyline is among the most anticholinergic"],["Bladder antispasmodics","Oxybutynin, tolterodine","Dry mouth is the most common side effect"],["Antipsychotics","Chlorpromazine, thioridazine, olanzapine, quetiapine","Dry mouth with low-potency and some atypical agents"],["Antiparkinson anticholinergics","Benztropine, trihexyphenidyl","Dry mouth, blurred vision, confusion"],["Antisecretory agents","Glycopyrrolate, scopolamine patch","Used on purpose to dry drooling or secretions; can thicken saliva"],["Inhaled anticholinergics","Tiotropium","Local dry mouth and throat"],["Muscle relaxants","Cyclobenzaprine, orphenadrine","Anticholinergic dry mouth plus sedation"]],"tip":"Anticholinergic burden is cumulative: several mild agents can equal one strong one."},{"h":"How Dry Mouth Affects Swallowing","cols":["Function","Role of saliva","What you may see"],"rows":[["Mastication","Moistens and softens food","Prolonged chewing, difficulty with dry or crumbly solids"],["Bolus formation","Binds particles into a cohesive bolus","Poor cohesion, scattered oral residue"],["Oral transit","Lubricates the tongue and palate","Slow transit, food sticking to the palate"],["Pharyngeal phase","Lubricates the pharynx","Sensation of food sticking, more liquid washes"],["Taste and appetite","Dissolves tastants","Altered taste, reduced intake"],["Oral health","Buffers acid and clears bacteria","Dental caries, candidiasis, mucositis risk"],["Speech","Lubricates articulators","Sticky articulation, complaints of effortful speech"]],"tip":"Alternating solids with liquid sips is a common compensation for xerostomia-related residue."},{"h":"Treating Xerostomia","p":"Treatment is either replacement (moisturizing) or stimulation of remaining salivary gland function.","cols":["Option","How it works","Key points"],"rows":[["Saliva substitutes (sprays, gels)","Coat and lubricate the mouth","Short-lived relief; useful before meals and at night"],["Sugar-free gum or lozenges","Mechanical and taste stimulation","Needs functioning glands; xylitol products protect teeth"],["Frequent water sips, humidifier","Replacement and comfort","Simple, low risk"],["Pilocarpine","Muscarinic agonist (sialogogue)","For radiation-induced xerostomia and Sjogren's; causes sweating"],["Cevimeline","Muscarinic agonist (sialogogue)","Approved for Sjogren's dry mouth; sweating, nausea"],["Medication review","Reduce anticholinergic load","Prescriber may find an alternative with less drying"]],"tip":"Sialogogues only work if some salivary tissue remains; substitutes work regardless."},{"h":"Antipsychotics, EPS and Tardive Dyskinesia","p":"Dopamine blockade can disrupt the oral and pharyngeal phases. Risk is highest with high-potency first-generation agents.","cols":["Effect","Timing","Swallowing and speech impact"],"rows":[["Acute dystonia","Hours to days after starting","Tongue protrusion, jaw or laryngeal spasm; can be an emergency"],["Drug-induced parkinsonism","Days to weeks","Reduced tongue movement, delayed swallow, hypophonia, drooling"],["Akathisia","Days to weeks","Restlessness disrupting meals and therapy"],["Tardive dyskinesia","Months to years","Involuntary tongue, lip and jaw movements; poor bolus control"],["Sedation","Any time","Reduced alertness during meals"],["Sialorrhea (clozapine)","Common, often at night","Drooling and pooling secretions"]],"tip":"Valbenazine and deutetrabenazine (VMAT2 inhibitors) are FDA-approved for tardive dyskinesia."},{"h":"Pill Esophagitis and Pill-Taking","p":"Some pills injure the esophagus if they lodge there. Risk rises with poor esophageal motility, lying down and low fluid intake.","cols":["Medicine","Concern","What to watch for or report"],"rows":[["Doxycycline","Acidic; classic cause of pill esophagitis","Painful swallowing after doses"],["Alendronate (bisphosphonates)","Esophageal irritation and ulcers","Label: full glass of water, upright 30 minutes"],["Potassium chloride tablets","Local mucosal injury","Chest pain or odynophagia"],["NSAIDs","Mucosal injury and ulcers","Heartburn, pain on swallowing"],["Ferrous sulfate","Mucosal irritation","Retrosternal pain"],["Extended-release tablets","Often cannot be crushed","Ask pharmacy before altering any pill"]],"tip":"Classic pill esophagitis: sudden odynophagia after taking a pill with little water or at bedtime."},{"h":"Botulinum Toxin and Spread","cols":["Use","SLP relevance","Report"],"rows":[["Cervical dystonia","Toxin can spread to pharyngeal muscles","New dysphagia days to weeks after injection"],["Spasmodic dysphonia","Breathy voice and mild aspiration after injection","Expected early; report if severe"],["Sialorrhea","Salivary gland injection reduces drooling","Thick saliva, chewing difficulty"],["Spasticity and limb injections","Distant spread is a boxed warning","Swallowing or breathing difficulty"],["Cricopharyngeal injection","Used for UES dysfunction","Transient worsening of dysphagia"]],"tip":"OnabotulinumtoxinA has a boxed warning for distant spread causing swallowing and breathing problems."},{"h":"Sedation, Cough and Aspiration","p":"Sedating medicines blunt alertness and protective cough, increasing silent aspiration risk.","cols":["Drug class","Examples","Effect on swallowing safety"],"rows":[["Benzodiazepines","Lorazepam, midazolam, alprazolam, clonazepam","Sedation, reduced pharyngeal coordination"],["Opioids","Morphine, hydromorphone, oxycodone, fentanyl","Cough suppression, sedation, delayed swallow"],["Antipsychotics","Haloperidol, quetiapine, olanzapine","Sedation plus EPS; aspiration pneumonia risk"],["Muscle relaxants","Baclofen, tizanidine, carisoprodol","Drowsiness and reduced tone"],["Hypnotics","Zolpidem, eszopiclone, suvorexant","Next-morning grogginess"],["Sedating antihistamines","Diphenhydramine, hydroxyzine","Sedation and dry mouth"],["Antiseizure medicines","Levetiracetam, topiramate, phenytoin","Drowsiness; topiramate word-finding difficulty"]],"tip":"Evaluate when the patient is most alert; note recent sedative doses in the report."},{"h":"Cognition, Voice and Communication","cols":["Medicine","Effect","SLP implication"],"rows":[["Anticholinergics in older adults","Confusion, memory loss, delirium","Beers Criteria flags; may mimic cognitive decline"],["Inhaled corticosteroids (fluticasone, budesonide)","Dysphonia, oral and laryngeal candidiasis","Report hoarseness; rinse mouth after use"],["ACE inhibitors (lisinopril)","Dry, persistent cough","Chronic cough can strain the voice"],["Pyridostigmine","Improves muscle strength in myasthenia gravis","Best function near peak effect; fatigue with use"],["Carbidopa/levodopa","Improves motor function","Speech and swallow better during 'on' time"],["Topiramate","Word-finding difficulty, slowed thinking","Can mimic aphasia-like symptoms"],["Cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil)","Modest cognitive benefit; may increase secretions","Note GI effects and appetite"],["Metoclopramide","EPS and tardive dyskinesia","Boxed warning for TD"]],"tip":"Myasthenia gravis: speech and swallowing worsen with repetition and later in the day."}]},"surg":{"tab":"Sterile Field Meds","sub":"Local anesthetics, hemostatics, irrigation, eye drugs, dyes and labeling for the CST","intro":"Surgical technologists receive, label and pass medications on the sterile field. This guide covers what each common field drug does, what to watch for, and the safety steps the CST exam expects.","sections":[{"h":"Local anesthetics at a glance","p":"Amides (lidocaine, bupivacaine) are the workhorses. Faster onset usually means shorter duration.","cols":["Drug","Onset / duration","Key points"],"rows":[["Lidocaine","Rapid onset (minutes); about 1-2 hours plain","Most common infiltration agent; also topical and IV regional (Bier) block"],["Bupivacaine","Slower onset; about 2-8 hours","Long-acting; most cardiotoxic; never used for Bier block"],["Bupivacaine liposomal","Lasts up to about 72 hours","Single-dose infiltration; do not mix with other local anesthetics on the field"],["Ropivacaine","Moderate onset; long duration","Less cardiotoxic than bupivacaine; common for nerve blocks"],["Mepivacaine","Rapid onset; intermediate duration","Common in dental and peripheral blocks"],["Chloroprocaine","Very rapid onset; short duration","Ester; broken down quickly in plasma"],["Tetracaine / proparacaine","Seconds; about 15-20 minutes","Topical esters used as eye drops"],["Articaine","Rapid onset","Dental local anesthetic, usually with epinephrine"]],"tip":"Amide names contain two letter i's (lidocaine, bupivacaine); esters have one (tetracaine, chloroprocaine)."},{"h":"With vs without epinephrine","p":"Epinephrine is added to many local anesthetics to constrict blood vessels at the site.","cols":["Effect or issue","What it means"],"rows":[["Vasoconstriction","Slows absorption so the anesthetic stays at the site"],["Longer duration","Block lasts noticeably longer than the plain drug"],["Less bleeding","Drier surgical field during incision and dissection"],["Lower toxicity risk","Slower absorption keeps blood levels lower"],["End-artery areas","Traditionally avoided in fingers, toes, nose, ears and penis"],["Bier block","Epinephrine-containing solutions are not used IV"],["Cardiac patients","Watch for tachycardia, hypertension and dysrhythmias"],["Labeling","Label must state 'with epinephrine'; plain and epi vials look alike"]],"tip":"When the surgeon asks for 'lidocaine with epi', verify both the drug and the epinephrine on the vial label aloud."},{"h":"Local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST)","p":"Toxicity happens when blood levels rise, often from accidental intravascular injection.","cols":["Stage","Signs to report"],"rows":[["Early CNS","Circumoral numbness, metallic taste, tinnitus, lightheadedness"],["Progressing CNS","Agitation, confusion, muscle twitching, visual changes"],["Severe CNS","Seizures, loss of consciousness"],["Cardiovascular","Bradycardia, dysrhythmias, hypotension, cardiac arrest"],["Highest risk drug","Bupivacaine (cardiotoxic)"],["Team response","Alert surgeon and anesthesia; lipid emulsion kit is the specific treatment"]],"tip":"Aspirating before injecting helps the surgeon avoid placing anesthetic in a vessel."},{"h":"Topical hemostatic agents","cols":["Agent (example)","How it works","Handling notes"],"rows":[["Topical thrombin (Thrombin-JMI, Recothrom, Evithrom)","Converts fibrinogen to fibrin to form a clot","Topical only; never injected; label clearly"],["Absorbable gelatin sponge (Gelfoam)","Matrix that traps platelets; swells","Used dry or soaked in saline or thrombin; absorbed in about 4-6 weeks"],["Oxidized regenerated cellulose (Surgicel)","Acidic mesh forms a gel clot","Applied dry; acidity inactivates thrombin; absorbed in about 1-2 weeks"],["Microfibrillar collagen (Avitene)","Attracts and activates platelets","Apply with dry gloves and instruments; keep out of cell-saver suction"],["Bone wax","Mechanical plug on bleeding bone","Not absorbed; can impair bone healing"],["Fibrin sealant","Thrombin plus fibrinogen form a fibrin clot","Mixed and applied as directed by the manufacturer"]],"tip":"Gelatin and cellulose both swell, so excess is removed near nerves, the spinal cord and bony canals."},{"h":"Irrigation solutions","cols":["Solution","Use","Watch for"],"rows":[["0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline)","Most common wound and cavity irrigation; isotonic","Conducts electricity, so not used with monopolar resectoscopes"],["Lactated Ringer's","Isotonic irrigation (e.g., arthroscopy)","Contains calcium and lactate"],["Sterile water","Rinsing instruments; some lyse tumor cells","Hypotonic: absorption causes hemolysis; avoided where large volumes can be absorbed"],["Glycine / sorbitol / mannitol","Non-conductive irrigation for monopolar TURP or hysteroscopy","Fluid absorption and hyponatremia"],["Antibiotic irrigation (bacitracin, gentamicin)","Added to saline in some implant and orthopedic cases","Check allergies; label basin with drug and strength"],["Warmed fluids","Irrigation is usually warmed to body temperature","Helps prevent hypothermia"]],"tip":"Bottles marked 'for irrigation' are not for injection; once opened, use or discard, never recap and save."},{"h":"Ophthalmic drugs","p":"Eye-drop caps follow a color code: mydriatics red, miotics green, anti-infectives tan, steroids pink.","cols":["Drug","Class / action","Notes"],"rows":[["Tropicamide","Anticholinergic: dilates pupil and paralyzes focusing","Short-acting (hours); red cap"],["Cyclopentolate","Anticholinergic mydriatic and cycloplegic","Longer-acting; red cap"],["Phenylephrine (eye)","Alpha agonist: dilates pupil only","No cycloplegia; can raise blood pressure"],["Proparacaine / tetracaine drops","Topical eye anesthetics","Work in seconds; repeated use damages the cornea"],["Acetylcholine intraocular (Miochol-E)","Miotic injected into anterior chamber","Constricts pupil within seconds; short-acting"],["Carbachol intraocular (Miostat)","Miotic injected into anterior chamber","Slower onset (minutes); lasts about a day"],["Hyaluronidase","Enzyme added to local anesthetic for eye blocks","Helps the anesthetic spread through tissue"]],"tip":"Mydriatics open the pupil before cataract surgery; miotics close it after the lens is placed."},{"h":"Dyes and anticoagulation on the field","cols":["Drug","Use","Watch for"],"rows":[["Methylene blue","Ureter, bladder or fistula identification; parathyroid","Serotonin syndrome with serotonergic drugs; falsely low pulse oximetry"],["Isosulfan blue (Lymphazurin)","Sentinel lymph node mapping","Anaphylaxis; blue urine; low pulse oximetry readings"],["Indocyanine green (ICG)","Fluorescent imaging of perfusion, bile ducts and nodes","Contains sodium iodide; ask about iodide allergy"],["Heparinized saline","Irrigating vessel lumens and catheters in vascular cases","Label clearly; never confuse with plain saline"],["Protamine","Reverses heparin at the end of vascular cases","Hypotension, anaphylaxis; risk with fish allergy or prior protamine-insulin use"]],"tip":"Two clear liquids on the back table (saline and heparinized saline) must each be labeled the moment they are poured."},{"h":"Medication safety on the sterile field","cols":["Step","What the CST does"],"rows":[["Receive","Circulator shows the container; both read name, strength and expiration aloud"],["Transfer","Use a sterile transfer device or pour; the circulator never reaches over the field"],["Label at once","Every syringe, cup and basin, even if only one drug is on the field"],["Label contents","Drug name, strength, and diluent or volume when relevant"],["Keep originals","Original vials stay in the room until the case ends"],["Pass","State the drug name and strength aloud when handing it to the surgeon"],["Unlabeled","Any unlabeled solution is discarded, never guessed"],["Shift change","Relief and departing staff review all field meds together"],["Allergies","Confirmed at time-out; affects antibiotics, dyes and irrigation"]],"tip":"The Joint Commission requires labeling all medications on and off the sterile field."},{"h":"Preoperative antibiotics","cols":["Drug","Timing before incision","Notes"],"rows":[["Cefazolin","Within 60 minutes","First choice for most clean cases; covers skin flora; redosed in long cases"],["Vancomycin","Within 120 minutes","Slow infusion; rapid infusion causes flushing reaction"],["Clindamycin","Within 60 minutes","Common alternative for severe beta-lactam allergy"],["Gentamicin","Within 60 minutes","Added for gram-negative coverage in some cases"]],"tip":"Vancomycin starts earlier because it must infuse slowly."}]},"anes":{"tab":"Anesthesia Cart","sub":"Induction agents, gases, blockers, MH, blocks and the emergency tray","intro":"A technologist's view of the drugs on and around the anesthesia workstation. Know what each agent does, what to watch for and how the carts are kept ready.","sections":[{"h":"IV induction and sedation agents","p":"These produce unconsciousness within about a minute of an IV bolus. The anesthesia provider chooses the agent; the tech should know why.","cols":["Agent","Key features","Watch for / tech notes"],"rows":[["Propofol","Milky lipid emulsion (soybean oil, egg lecithin); onset under a minute, wakes in minutes; antiemetic","Hypotension, apnea, injection pain; supports bacterial growth, so strict asepsis and timely discard"],["Etomidate","Keeps blood pressure and heart rate stable; good for unstable patients","Myoclonus, nausea, injection pain; brief adrenal (cortisol) suppression"],["Ketamine","NMDA antagonist; dissociative; keeps breathing and airway reflexes; bronchodilates","Raises heart rate and BP; more secretions; emergence hallucinations"],["Methohexital","Short-acting barbiturate; common choice for ECT","Does not blunt seizures much; hiccups, myoclonus; avoid in porphyria"],["Midazolam","Benzodiazepine; anxiolysis and amnesia","Respiratory depression with opioids; reversed by flumazenil"],["Remimazolam","Ultra-short benzodiazepine broken down by tissue esterases","Hypotension, hypoxia; flumazenil reverses"],["Dexmedetomidine","Alpha-2 agonist; sedation with little respiratory depression","Bradycardia and hypotension"]],"tip":"Ketamine for the hypotensive asthmatic; etomidate for hemodynamic stability; methohexital for ECT."},{"h":"Inhaled anesthetics","p":"Potency is described by MAC: the alveolar concentration that stops movement to incision in half of patients. Lower MAC means more potent.","cols":["Agent","Key traits","Tech notes"],"rows":[["Sevoflurane","Sweet, nonpungent; agent of choice for mask induction, especially in children","MH trigger; compound A forms with CO2 absorbent at low fresh gas flows"],["Desflurane","Lowest blood-gas solubility of the volatiles: fastest wake-up","Pungent airway irritant; needs heated, pressurized vaporizer (boils near room temp); MH trigger"],["Isoflurane","Most potent of the three volatiles (lowest MAC); pungent","MH trigger; slower emergence than sevo or des"],["Nitrous oxide","Weak alone; speeds uptake of other agents (second gas effect)","Not an MH trigger; expands closed air spaces; supports combustion"],["Nitrous oxide cylinder","Stored as liquid in a blue cylinder (US)","Gauge stays steady until liquid is gone; weigh to estimate contents"],["Diffusion hypoxia","Nitrous oxide leaving blood dilutes alveolar oxygen at emergence","Prevented with high-flow oxygen for several minutes at the end"]],"tip":"Potency: isoflurane > sevoflurane > desflurane > nitrous oxide. Speed of wake-up runs the other way."},{"h":"Opioids and reversal","cols":["Agent","Profile","Watch for"],"rows":[["Fentanyl","Synthetic, about 100 times morphine; onset 1-2 minutes, lasts 30-60 minutes","Chest wall rigidity with rapid large boluses; apnea; accumulates with long infusions"],["Remifentanil","Broken down by blood and tissue esterases; offset in minutes even after long infusions","Needs a plan for postop pain; clear the IV line so leftover drug is not bolused later; not for neuraxial use"],["Sufentanil / alfentanil","Sufentanil very potent; alfentanil fastest onset","Respiratory depression, rigidity, bradycardia"],["Hydromorphone / morphine","Longer-acting opioids for postop pain","Sedation, respiratory depression; morphine releases histamine"],["Naloxone","Opioid antagonist","Acute withdrawal, pain, hypertension, pulmonary edema; may wear off before the opioid"]]},{"h":"Neuromuscular blockers","p":"Blockers paralyze but give no sedation or pain relief, so awareness is a risk if anesthetic depth is inadequate.","cols":["Agent","Class / duration","Key points"],"rows":[["Succinylcholine","Depolarizing; onset under a minute, lasts about 5-10 minutes","Fasciculations, hyperkalemia, MH trigger, masseter spasm; broken down by plasma cholinesterase; refrigerated"],["Rocuronium","Aminosteroid nondepolarizer; intermediate","Fastest-onset nondepolarizer, so used for RSI; reversed by sugammadex; refrigerated"],["Vecuronium","Aminosteroid nondepolarizer; intermediate","Powder that must be reconstituted; sugammadex reverses"],["Cisatracurium","Benzylisoquinoline nondepolarizer; intermediate","Hofmann elimination, organ independent; little histamine release; refrigerated; not reversed by sugammadex"],["Atracurium","Benzylisoquinoline nondepolarizer; intermediate","Histamine release (flushing, hypotension)"],["Pancuronium","Aminosteroid nondepolarizer; long acting","Vagolytic tachycardia; renally cleared"]],"tip":"Succinylcholine hyperkalemia risk: burns, crush injury, denervation, prolonged immobility and undiagnosed myopathy in children (boxed warning)."},{"h":"Reversal and train-of-four monitoring","cols":["Item","What it is","Key points"],"rows":[["Sugammadex","Modified cyclodextrin that encapsulates rocuronium or vecuronium","Works even in deep block; no anticholinergic needed; reduces hormonal contraceptive effect for 7 days; bradycardia, anaphylaxis"],["Neostigmine","Cholinesterase inhibitor; raises acetylcholine","Ceiling effect: needs some twitches back; peak about 10 minutes"],["Glycopyrrolate","Anticholinergic paired with neostigmine","Blocks bradycardia and secretions; does not cross into the brain"],["Edrophonium","Fast-onset cholinesterase inhibitor","Paired with atropine because onsets match"],["Train-of-four (TOF)","Four stimuli over two seconds, usually ulnar nerve, watching thumb adduction","Fade (4th weaker than 1st) means nondepolarizing block"],["TOF ratio","4th twitch height divided by 1st","0.9 or more, measured quantitatively, indicates adequate recovery"],["Facial nerve site","Eyebrow muscles","Recovers sooner than the thumb, so may overestimate recovery"],["Post-tetanic count","Twitches counted after a tetanic burst","Gauges deep block when TOF shows zero"]],"tip":"Phase I succinylcholine block: all twitches reduced equally, no fade."},{"h":"Malignant hyperthermia","p":"An inherited skeletal muscle disorder (often RYR1) in which triggers cause uncontrolled calcium release and a runaway hypermetabolic state.","cols":["Topic","Key facts","Tech focus"],"rows":[["Triggers","All volatile agents (sevo, iso, des) and succinylcholine","Know which drugs are safe: propofol, nitrous oxide, opioids, benzodiazepines, ketamine, nondepolarizers, local anesthetics"],["Early signs","Unexplained rising end-tidal CO2, tachycardia, masseter or generalized rigidity","Report rising EtCO2 despite more ventilation"],["Later signs","Hyperthermia, arrhythmias, cola-colored urine","Temperature rise is often late"],["Labs","Mixed acidosis, hyperkalemia, rising CK, myoglobin","Help draw and run ABG, potassium, CK, coags, urine myoglobin"],["Dantrolene","Blocks calcium release from muscle ryanodine receptor","Reconstitute with sterile water without preservative, not saline or dextrose"],["Dantrium / Revonto vs Ryanodex","Older forms need many vials and large water volumes; Ryanodex needs far less","Mixing older forms takes extra hands"],["Circuit and machine","Volatiles turned off; hyperventilate with high-flow oxygen","Place activated charcoal filters on both circuit limbs"],["Cooling","Cold IV saline, ice packs to groin, axillae and neck","Gather ice and cold fluids; cooling ends near normal temperature"],["Drug interaction","Calcium channel blockers with dantrolene risk hyperkalemia and collapse","Know what is on the MH cart and why"],["After the crisis","ICU monitoring at least 24 hours for recrudescence; MHAUS registry report","Restock MH cart immediately"],["MH-susceptible patient prep","Flush machine per maker; remove or tape vaporizers; new circuit and absorbent; charcoal filters","Follow the facility MH machine-prep checklist"]],"tip":"MHAUS hotline gives 24-hour expert advice during a crisis."},{"h":"Local anesthetics and LAST","cols":["Item","Key facts","Watch for"],"rows":[["Amides","Lidocaine, bupivacaine, ropivacaine, mepivacaine, prilocaine, articaine (two i's)","Liver metabolism; true allergy rare"],["Esters","Chloroprocaine, tetracaine, benzocaine","Plasma cholinesterase; PABA-related allergy"],["Bupivacaine","Long acting; most cardiotoxic","Cardiac arrest from LAST is hard to resuscitate"],["Ropivacaine","Long acting; less cardiotoxic, less motor block","Still causes LAST"],["Liposomal bupivacaine","Extended release for up to about 72 hours","Other local anesthetics like lidocaine nearby can release bupivacaine quickly; separate timing"],["Prilocaine, benzocaine","Topical and dental use","Methemoglobinemia (cyanosis, low SpO2)"],["Epinephrine additive","Prolongs block, lowers absorption","Rising heart rate can signal intravascular injection"],["LAST: CNS","Perioral numbness, metallic taste, tinnitus, agitation, seizures","Report early symptoms right away"],["LAST: heart","Hypertension and tachycardia, then bradycardia, arrhythmia, arrest","Can be delayed; monitoring continues"],["LAST treatment","Airway and oxygen, benzodiazepines for seizures, lipid emulsion 20%","Modified ACLS: smaller epinephrine doses; avoid vasopressin, beta blockers, local anesthetics"]],"tip":"A LAST kit with lipid emulsion belongs wherever blocks are done."},{"h":"Emergency and vasoactive drugs","cols":["Drug","Action","Typical use / watch for"],"rows":[["Phenylephrine","Pure alpha-1 agonist","Hypotension with fast heart rate; reflex bradycardia"],["Ephedrine","Mixed direct and indirect alpha/beta","Hypotension with slow heart rate; tachyphylaxis"],["Atropine","Anticholinergic; crosses into the brain","Symptomatic bradycardia; tachycardia, dry mouth, confusion"],["Epinephrine","Alpha and beta agonist","Anaphylaxis, cardiac arrest; stocked in more than one concentration"],["Norepinephrine / vasopressin","Potent vasoconstrictors","Refractory hypotension, vasoplegia; extravasation injury"],["Calcium chloride","Calcium replacement","Hyperkalemia, calcium channel blocker toxicity; best via central line"],["Adenosine","Briefly blocks AV node","SVT; rapid push with flush; brief asystole"],["Esmolol / labetalol","Beta blockers (labetalol also alpha)","Tachycardia, hypertension; esmolol is ultra-short"],["Amiodarone","Antiarrhythmic","VF/VT, atrial fibrillation; hypotension"],["Sodium bicarbonate","Buffer","Severe acidosis, hyperkalemia"]]},{"h":"Organizing and restocking the cart","p":"A standardized cart lets anyone find the right drug fast. The same layout should be used in every room.","cols":["Practice","Why it matters","Tech detail"],"rows":[["Color-coded labels (ASTM D4774)","Drug class at a glance","Induction yellow, opioids blue, NMBs fluorescent red, vasopressors violet, local anesthetics gray, anticholinergics green, benzodiazepines orange"],["White diagonal stripes","Marks an antagonist of that class","Naloxone blue striped; neostigmine red striped; flumazenil orange striped"],["Segregate paralytics","Prevents mix-ups with look-alikes","Warning caps; separate bin"],["Look-alike / sound-alike","Wrong-drug errors","Separate them; tall man lettering; check concentration"],["Refrigerated drugs","Succinylcholine, rocuronium, cisatracurium lose shelf life at room temp","Label date out of fridge and new beyond-use date; check fridge temperature log"],["Expiration rotation","Avoids expired stock","New stock behind old; first-to-expire used first"],["Numbered breakaway seals","Show whether cart was opened","Record seal number on the check log"],["Controlled substances","Diversion prevention","Locked or automated dispensing; witnessed waste; count reconciled"],["Syringe labeling","Unlabeled syringes cause errors","Label every syringe not used at once: drug, strength, expiration"],["Emergency carts (code, MH, LAST)","Must be complete when needed","Check on schedule and restock immediately after use"]],"tip":"Single-dose vials are for one patient only."}]},"dent":{"tab":"Dental Pharmacology","sub":"Local anesthetics, oral drug effects, bleeding risk and prophylaxis for dental hygiene","intro":"The medicines dental hygienists and assistants meet most in the chair, and what to look for on the medication history. Organized around NBDHE-style topics.","sections":[{"h":"Local anesthetics at a glance","p":"Amides (an extra \"i\" before -caine) are metabolized mainly by the liver; esters are broken down by plasma pseudocholinesterase.","cols":["Agent","Class / key feature","Watch for"],"rows":[["Lidocaine","Amide prototype; rapid onset, intermediate duration","Liver disease slows clearance"],["Articaine","Amide with thiophene ring and ester side chain; partly broken down in plasma","Paresthesia reports after mandibular blocks; methemoglobinemia"],["Mepivacaine","Amide; only mild vasodilation, so works without vasoconstrictor","Short pulpal duration when plain"],["Prilocaine","Amide; metabolite o-toluidine","Methemoglobinemia, especially with congenital or acquired risk"],["Bupivacaine","Long-acting amide; used for long procedures and postoperative pain","Prolonged numbness, lip and cheek biting; most cardiotoxic"],["Benzocaine","Ester topical","Methemoglobinemia; FDA warns against teething use under age 2"],["Tetracaine","Potent ester topical","Rapid absorption from mucosa"],["Ester agents","Metabolized to PABA","True allergy is more likely with esters than amides"]],"tip":"Articaine is the amide that is partly metabolized in plasma, giving it a short half-life."},{"h":"Vasoconstrictors and interactions","p":"Epinephrine is added to slow absorption from the injection site.","cols":["Point","What it means"],"rows":[["Why epinephrine is added","Slows systemic absorption, lowers toxicity risk, lengthens duration, improves hemostasis"],["Cardiovascular disease","Use the least vasoconstrictor needed; aspirate and inject slowly; monitor vitals"],["Uncontrolled hypertension or recent MI","Elective care is deferred; medical consult first"],["Nonselective beta-blockers (propranolol)","Unopposed alpha effect: sharp rise in BP with reflex bradycardia"],["Cardioselective beta-blockers (metoprolol)","Interaction much less likely"],["Tricyclic antidepressants (amitriptyline)","Block norepinephrine reuptake; exaggerated pressor response, avoid levonordefrin"],["Hyperthyroidism (uncontrolled)","Increased sensitivity to epinephrine"],["Epinephrine reaction vs anxiety","Transient tachycardia, palpitations, tremor, anxiety soon after injection"]],"tip":"Propranolol plus epinephrine: hypertension with a slowing heart rate."},{"h":"Anesthetic toxicity and methemoglobinemia","cols":["Problem","Early signs","Later signs / notes"],"rows":[["Local anesthetic overdose (CNS)","Circumoral numbness, metallic taste, tinnitus, slurred speech, talkativeness","Muscle twitching, seizures, then CNS and respiratory depression"],["Local anesthetic overdose (CV)","Initial rise in HR and BP","Hypotension, bradycardia, arrhythmia, arrest"],["Common causes","Intravascular injection, repeated injections, small body weight","Aspiration and slow injection reduce risk"],["Methemoglobinemia","Gray-blue cyanosis not improved by oxygen, low pulse oximetry","Chocolate-brown blood; treated with methylene blue"],["Methemoglobinemia triggers","Prilocaine, articaine, benzocaine","Higher risk: infants, congenital disease, other oxidizing drugs"],["Allergy","Hives, itching, bronchospasm","Rare with amides; often a sulfite (antioxidant) or epinephrine reaction"]],"tip":"Toxicity is excitation first, then depression."},{"h":"Drug-induced gingival overgrowth","p":"Fibrous enlargement usually starts in the interdental papillae, most often anterior and facial.","cols":["Drug","Class / use","Notes"],"rows":[["Phenytoin","Anticonvulsant","Classic cause; common in young patients"],["Cyclosporine","Immunosuppressant after transplant","Often seen with a calcium channel blocker too"],["Nifedipine","Calcium channel blocker","Most often cited CCB"],["Amlodipine, diltiazem, verapamil","Calcium channel blockers","Same effect, less frequent"],["Contributing factor","Plaque and inflammation","Meticulous plaque control lessens severity"],["Management","Frequent recall, debridement","Surgical reduction may recur; prescriber may consider alternatives"]],"tip":"Three P-C-C causes: Phenytoin, Cyclosporine, Calcium channel blockers."},{"h":"Xerostomia, staining and other oral effects","cols":["Effect","Common causes","Hygiene focus"],"rows":[["Xerostomia","Anticholinergics (oxybutynin), antihistamines, TCAs, opioids, diuretics","Root and cervical caries risk; fluoride and frequent recall"],["Sialogogues","Pilocarpine, cevimeline (muscarinic agonists)","Sweating, flushing; caution in asthma and narrow-angle glaucoma"],["Oral candidiasis","Inhaled corticosteroids, broad-spectrum antibiotics, xerostomia","Rinse and spit after inhaler; spacer use"],["Antifungal options","Nystatin, clotrimazole, fluconazole","Fluconazole raises INR with warfarin"],["Intrinsic tooth staining","Tetracycline during tooth development; minocycline even in adults","Tetracyclines avoided in pregnancy and young children"],["Extrinsic staining","Chlorhexidine, stannous fluoride","Removable by polishing"],["Taste change","Metronidazole (metallic), chlorhexidine","Report persistent changes"]]},{"h":"Medication-related osteonecrosis of the jaw","p":"MRONJ is exposed or probeable bone in the maxillofacial region for more than 8 weeks in a patient on antiresorptive or antiangiogenic therapy, without jaw radiation.","cols":["Item","Key facts"],"rows":[["Bisphosphonates","Alendronate (oral, osteoporosis); zoledronic acid (IV, cancer and osteoporosis)"],["Denosumab","RANKL inhibitor; does not bind bone, effect fades over months"],["Highest risk","IV therapy for cancer (bone metastases, myeloma) vs low risk with oral osteoporosis therapy"],["Main trigger","Dentoalveolar surgery such as extractions"],["Location","Mandible more often than maxilla"],["Prevention","Dental evaluation and needed treatment before therapy begins; good oral hygiene"],["Signs to report","Exposed bone, pain, swelling, loose teeth, numbness, draining fistula"],["Bisphosphonate persistence","Bound to bone for years after therapy ends"]],"tip":"Ask about osteoporosis and cancer drugs, including IV and injectable ones patients may forget."},{"h":"Bleeding risk and anticoagulants","p":"Most dental care, including scaling, can proceed without interrupting anticoagulants; decisions belong to the dentist and prescriber.","cols":["Drug","Mechanism","Lab / dental notes"],"rows":[["Warfarin","Vitamin K antagonist","INR checked close to the procedure (ideally within 24 hours)"],["Apixaban, rivaroxaban","Factor Xa inhibitors (DOACs)","No routine INR; short half-life, bleeding varies with dose timing"],["Dabigatran","Direct thrombin inhibitor","INR not useful; renal clearance"],["Heparin","Enhances antithrombin","Monitored with aPTT; usually hospital setting"],["Aspirin","Irreversible COX-1 inhibitor in platelets","Effect lasts the platelet lifespan (7-10 days)"],["Clopidogrel","Irreversible P2Y12 inhibitor","Often dual therapy after stent; interruption risks clotting"],["Interactions raising INR","Metronidazole, fluconazole, some macrolides","Report new prescriptions to the dentist"],["Analgesics","NSAIDs add bleeding risk","Acetaminophen usually preferred for warfarin users"],["Local hemostasis","Pressure with gauze, sutures","Oxidized cellulose, gelatin sponge, tranexamic acid rinse"]],"tip":"Common therapeutic INR is 2-3; ask when it was last checked."},{"h":"Antibiotic prophylaxis (AHA)","p":"Prophylaxis is a single dose taken 30-60 minutes before procedures that manipulate gingiva, the periapical region, or perforate mucosa.","cols":["Category","Examples"],"rows":[["Recommended: prosthetic valve","Prosthetic valve or prosthetic material used for valve repair"],["Recommended: previous endocarditis","Any prior infective endocarditis"],["Recommended: congenital heart disease","Unrepaired cyanotic CHD; repaired with prosthetic material in first 6 months; residual defect near patch"],["Recommended: transplant","Cardiac transplant recipient with valve disease"],["Not recommended","Mitral valve prolapse, murmurs, stents, CABG, pacemakers"],["Joint replacement","ADA: generally not recommended"],["First-line","Amoxicillin"],["Penicillin allergy","Cephalexin (no anaphylaxis history), azithromycin, clarithromycin, doxycycline"],["No longer recommended","Clindamycin (2021 AHA update)"],["Missed dose","May be given up to 2 hours after the procedure"]],"tip":"Risk depends on the heart condition, not on the dental procedure alone."},{"h":"Dental infections, chlorhexidine and fluoride","cols":["Agent","Use","Key notes"],"rows":[["Amoxicillin / penicillin VK","First-line for odontogenic infections","Adjunct to drainage or endodontic treatment"],["Metronidazole","Anaerobes, necrotizing periodontal disease","Disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol; raises INR"],["Clindamycin","Penicillin allergy","Highest C. difficile risk"],["Doxycycline","Subantimicrobial dose for periodontitis","Inhibits collagenase; chelated by calcium and antacids"],["Minocycline microspheres","Local delivery into pockets","Adjunct to scaling and root planing"],["Antibiotics not indicated","Irreversible pulpitis or localized abscess without systemic signs","Definitive dental treatment is the answer"],["Chlorhexidine","Plaque and gingivitis rinse","Substantivity; brown stain, taste change, more calculus"],["Chlorhexidine timing","Separate from toothpaste","Sodium lauryl sulfate inactivates it"],["Fluoride","Forms fluorapatite, promotes remineralization","Inhibits bacterial enzymes; varnish for high caries risk"],["Acidulated phosphate fluoride","Professional gel","Can etch porcelain and composite restorations"],["Fluorosis","Excess fluoride while enamel forms","Risk window is early childhood"]],"tip":"Fever, swelling spreading, trismus or lymphadenopathy suggest systemic spread."}]},"phleb":{"tab":"Draws & Drug Levels","sub":"Blood thinners, drug-level timing and IV interference at the draw","intro":"Many patients you stick are on medicines that change how they bleed or what their results mean. This guide covers anticoagulant draws, timed drug levels and the IV and additive errors that ruin a specimen.","sections":[{"h":"Blood thinners at the bedside","p":"Anticoagulants slow clotting; antiplatelets keep platelets from plugging the puncture. Either one means the site can keep oozing.","cols":["Medicine","Type","What it means for the draw"],"rows":[["Warfarin","Vitamin K antagonist (oral)","Prolonged bleeding; followed with PT/INR"],["Heparin (unfractionated)","Indirect thrombin and Xa inhibitor (IV/subQ)","Followed with aPTT or anti-Xa; never draw above the drip"],["Enoxaparin, dalteparin","Low-molecular-weight heparin (subQ)","Bruising common; anti-Xa if monitoring is ordered"],["Apixaban, rivaroxaban, edoxaban","Oral factor Xa inhibitors (DOACs)","Usually no routine level; still bleed longer"],["Dabigatran","Oral direct thrombin inhibitor","Can prolong aPTT and thrombin time"],["Aspirin, clopidogrel, prasugrel, ticagrelor","Antiplatelets","Platelet plug slower; site may ooze"],["Tirofiban","IV glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitor","Often given with heparin; high bleeding risk"],["Alteplase, tenecteplase, reteplase","Thrombolytics (clot busters)","Very high bleeding risk; limit punctures, long pressure"]],"tip":"Ask about blood thinners before the stick so you plan for extra pressure, not after the site is bleeding."},{"h":"Protecting the site","p":"The goal is a sealed puncture and no hematoma before the patient leaves.","cols":["Step","Why"],"rows":[["Ask about anticoagulants and antiplatelets","Plan for longer pressure and closer site check"],["Choose the best vein on the first try","Each extra puncture is another bleeding site"],["Apply firm pressure with gauze, arm straight","Bending the elbow traps blood and causes a hematoma"],["Hold pressure until bleeding fully stops","Thinned blood may need several extra minutes"],["Check the site before bandaging","A bandage over an oozing site hides a growing hematoma"],["Report bleeding that will not stop","The nurse or provider must assess it"],["Avoid arterial punctures unless trained and ordered","Arterial sites bleed much more on thinners"]],"tip":"Never leave a patient whose site is still bleeding; notify the nurse."},{"h":"Coag tests and the light blue tube","p":"Coagulation tests use 3.2% sodium citrate in a fixed 9 to 1 blood-to-additive ratio, so fill volume matters.","cols":["Test","Watches","Tube and handling notes"],"rows":[["PT/INR","Warfarin","Light blue citrate; fill to the line"],["aPTT","Unfractionated heparin","Light blue; process within about 1 hour"],["Anti-Xa","Heparin, LMWH, sometimes DOACs","Light blue; timing to dose recorded"],["Thrombin time","Heparin, dabigatran effect","Light blue citrate"],["Platelet function tests","Aspirin, P2Y12 inhibitors","Citrate tube per lab; handle gently"],["Underfilled tube","Too much citrate for the blood","Falsely prolonged results; recollect"],["Butterfly with coag first","Tubing air underfills the tube","Draw a discard tube first"],["Mixing","Activates or clots the sample if rough","Gently invert 3 to 4 times"]],"tip":"Light blue tubes must be filled to the line; a short draw is rejected."},{"h":"Peak and trough basics","p":"A drug level only means something when the lab knows when it was drawn relative to the last dose.","cols":["Term","Meaning","Usual timing"],"rows":[["Trough","Lowest level","Just before the next dose (within about 30 minutes)"],["Peak","Highest level","Set time after the dose or infusion ends"],["Random level","Any time","Used when timing is not critical or toxicity suspected"],["Steady state","Level is stable dose to dose","Often after several doses; lab or pharmacy decides"],["Timed draw","Exact time ordered","Draw on time; document actual time"],["Late or early draw","Level misread","Notify the nurse; document the true time"]],"tip":"Record collection time AND last dose time; without them the level cannot be interpreted."},{"h":"Commonly monitored drugs","p":"Pharmacy and the nurse set timing; the phlebotomist draws on time and documents.","cols":["Drug","Usual timing","Draw notes"],"rows":[["Vancomycin","Trough before dose; sometimes peak after infusion","Draw from arm without the infusion"],["Gentamicin, tobramycin, amikacin","Peak about 30 minutes after infusion ends; trough before dose","Kidney and hearing toxicity drive monitoring"],["Digoxin","At least 6 to 8 hours after the dose","Earlier draws read falsely high"],["Lithium","About 12 hours after last dose","Never in a lithium heparin tube"],["Phenytoin","Trough before dose","Low albumin may need a free level"],["Valproic acid","Trough before dose","Highly protein bound; free level sometimes"],["Carbamazepine","Trough before dose","Draw time and dose time documented"],["Theophylline","Per order, often trough","Narrow range; many interactions"],["Tacrolimus, cyclosporine","Trough before dose","Whole blood in lavender EDTA"],["Methotrexate","Timed after high-dose infusion","Exact timing guides rescue therapy"]],"tip":"Some labs want drug levels in a red top without gel, since gel can absorb certain drugs."},{"h":"IV lines and contamination","p":"Blood drawn above or near a running IV is diluted by the fluid and picks up whatever is infusing.","cols":["Infusing fluid or drug","Falsely changes","Clue"],"rows":[["Dextrose","Glucose very high","Glucose high without diabetes history"],["Normal saline","Sodium and chloride high; other values diluted","Low hemoglobin, protein, calcium together"],["Potassium chloride","Potassium high","High potassium without hemolysis"],["Lactated Ringer's","Lactate, calcium raised; others diluted","Unexpected lactate rise"],["Heparin drip or flush","aPTT and anti-Xa falsely prolonged","Coag results way off from prior"],["Regular insulin drip","Glucose falsely low or erratic","Glucose drop not matching patient"],["Drug infusing (e.g., vancomycin)","Drug level falsely very high","Level far above expected"]],"tip":"Use the arm opposite the IV; if none, draw below the IV and document the site."},{"h":"Choosing the site with an IV","p":"Follow facility policy and document anything unusual.","cols":["Situation","Best practice"],"rows":[["IV in one arm","Draw from the other arm"],["IVs in both arms","Draw below an IV, never above; nurse decides on interrupting the infusion"],["Line or port draw needed","Done by trained nurse with discard per protocol"],["Heparin lock or flushed line","Discard volume prevents heparin contamination"],["Arm with fistula or lymphedema","Avoid; use another site per policy"],["Hand IV in place","Do not draw above it in the same arm"],["Requisition note","Record site and that an IV was present"]],"tip":"Drawing below an IV is a last resort, never above it."},{"h":"Order of draw and other interferences","p":"Additives carried from one tube to the next on the needle can change results.","cols":["Tube or factor","Effect if mishandled"],"rows":[["1. Blood cultures","Drawn first to keep them sterile"],["2. Light blue citrate","Coags before clot activator or heparin can enter"],["3. Red / gold serum","Clot activator into citrate shortens clotting times"],["4. Green heparin","Heparin carryover prolongs coags"],["5. Lavender EDTA","EDTA carryover: potassium high, calcium low"],["6. Gray fluoride/oxalate","Last; oxalate and fluoride alter enzymes and electrolytes"],["Biotin supplements","Falsely high or low results on some immunoassays (troponin, TSH, hCG)"],["Hemolysis","Falsely high potassium, LDH, AST"],["Prolonged tourniquet","Hemoconcentration; keep to about 1 minute"]],"tip":"Ask about high-dose biotin when the lab requires it, and record it."}]},"ekg":{"tab":"Drugs on the Strip","sub":"How common medicines and electrolytes change rate, intervals and rhythm","intro":"Many rhythm changes you see on a monitor are caused by medicines or electrolytes. Know the pattern, know the likely cause, and know what to report right away.","sections":[{"h":"Drugs that slow rate and conduction","p":"These medicines slow the SA node, the AV node, or both. Expect slower rates and longer PR intervals.","cols":["Drug or class","Examples","What you may see on the strip"],"rows":[["Beta-blockers","Metoprolol, atenolol, carvedilol, propranolol, esmolol","Sinus bradycardia, longer PR, AV block"],["Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","Diltiazem, verapamil","Slower ventricular rate in A-fib, bradycardia, AV block"],["Digoxin","Digoxin","Slower ventricular rate in A-fib, longer PR"],["Amiodarone","Amiodarone","Sinus bradycardia, longer PR and QT"],["Ivabradine","Ivabradine","Slower sinus rate; may cause bradycardia"],["Beta-blocker plus calcium channel blocker","Metoprolol with diltiazem","Additive bradycardia and heart block risk"],["Donepezil","Donepezil","Bradycardia; can contribute to syncope"]],"tip":"New bradycardia or a dropped beat pattern after a rate-control medicine should be reported."},{"h":"Drugs that speed rate or open conduction","cols":["Drug","How it works","Strip clue"],"rows":[["Atropine","Blocks vagal tone at SA and AV nodes","Faster sinus rate; first drug for symptomatic bradycardia"],["Isoproterenol","Beta-agonist; raises heart rate","Faster rate; used for some bradycardias and torsades"],["Epinephrine","Alpha and beta stimulation","Faster rate; can trigger ectopy"],["Dopamine","Raises rate and contractility","Sinus tachycardia, PVCs possible"]],"tip":"Atropine often does little for blocks below the AV node (Mobitz II, third-degree); pacing may be needed."},{"h":"Adenosine for SVT","p":"Adenosine briefly blocks the AV node. It is used to stop stable narrow-complex reentrant SVT.","cols":["Feature","What to know"],"rows":[["Onset and duration","Works within seconds; half-life under ten seconds"],["Expected strip","Brief pause or asystole for a few seconds, then sinus rhythm"],["Why record a strip","A running strip captures conversion or unmasks flutter waves"],["Patient feeling","Chest pressure, flushing, sense of doom; passes quickly"],["Flutter or A-fib","Rate slows briefly, revealing flutter or fibrillatory waves"],["Interactions","Caffeine and theophylline blunt it; dipyridamole strengthens it"]],"tip":"A few seconds of asystole after adenosine is expected; prolonged asystole is not."},{"h":"QT-prolonging medicines","p":"These drugs lengthen ventricular repolarization. A long QT raises the risk of torsades de pointes.","cols":["Group","Examples"],"rows":[["Class III antiarrhythmics","Amiodarone, sotalol, dofetilide, ibutilide, dronedarone"],["Class IA antiarrhythmics","Procainamide, disopyramide"],["Antiemetics","Ondansetron, droperidol"],["Antipsychotics","Haloperidol, ziprasidone, quetiapine"],["Opioid","Methadone"],["Macrolide antibiotics","Azithromycin"],["Fluoroquinolones","Levofloxacin, moxifloxacin, ciprofloxacin"],["Antidepressants","Citalopram, escitalopram, amitriptyline"],["Other","Hydroxychloroquine"]],"tip":"Two QT-prolonging drugs plus low potassium or magnesium is a classic torsades setup."},{"h":"Reading the QT and spotting torsades","cols":["Item","What to know"],"rows":[["QT interval","Start of QRS to end of T wave"],["QTc","QT corrected for heart rate; most monitors calculate it"],["Quick check","QT longer than half the R-R interval suggests a long QT at normal rates"],["Concerning QTc","Above about 500 ms, or a rise over 60 ms from baseline"],["Sotalol and dofetilide","Started with days of telemetry and repeated QTc checks"],["Torsades look","Polymorphic VT that twists around the baseline"],["Torsades setting","Long QT, slow rate, pauses, low potassium or magnesium"],["Common treatment","Magnesium sulfate; defibrillation if pulseless"]],"tip":"Measure QT in the same lead each time, usually lead II or V5, so trends are meaningful."},{"h":"Digoxin: effect vs toxicity","cols":["Finding","Effect or toxicity","Notes"],"rows":[["Scooped, sagging ST (\"Salvador Dali mustache\")","Effect","Expected at therapeutic levels"],["Shorter QT, flat or inverted T","Effect","Not by itself a sign of toxicity"],["Frequent PVCs, bigeminy","Toxicity","Common early toxic rhythm"],["AV block, any degree","Toxicity","Report new or worsening block"],["Atrial tachycardia with block","Toxicity","Classic digoxin toxic rhythm"],["Regularized slow A-fib (junctional)","Toxicity","A-fib suddenly becomes regular"],["Bidirectional VT","Toxicity","Rare but very specific"],["Nausea, vision changes (yellow-green halos)","Toxicity","Symptoms to report with the strip"]],"tip":"Low potassium, low magnesium, high calcium and kidney problems all raise digoxin toxicity risk."},{"h":"Electrolytes on the strip","cols":["Imbalance","Strip findings","Common drug links"],"rows":[["Hyperkalemia, early","Tall, narrow, peaked T waves","Spironolactone, lisinopril, potassium chloride"],["Hyperkalemia, worsening","Flat or absent P, long PR, wide QRS","Kidney failure raises risk"],["Hyperkalemia, severe","Sine-wave pattern, VF, asystole","Calcium protects the heart muscle"],["Hypokalemia","U waves, flat T, ST depression, ectopy","Furosemide and other loop diuretics"],["Hypomagnesemia","Long QT, torsades, ectopy","Diuretics; often with low potassium"],["Hypocalcemia","Long QT from long ST segment","Treated with calcium chloride or gluconate"],["Hypercalcemia","Short QT","Raises digoxin toxicity risk"]],"tip":"Peaked T waves plus a widening QRS is an emergency: report immediately."},{"h":"What to report immediately","cols":["Finding","Why it matters"],"rows":[["New second-degree Mobitz II or third-degree block","Can progress to asystole"],["Symptomatic bradycardia","May need atropine or pacing"],["QTc above about 500 ms or a large jump","Torsades risk"],["Torsades, VT, or VF","Life-threatening; call for help"],["Peaked T waves or a widening QRS","Possible severe hyperkalemia"],["New ectopy in a patient on digoxin","Possible digoxin toxicity"],["Pause longer than expected after adenosine","Prolonged asystole needs response"],["Frequent or new runs of PVCs","May reflect electrolytes or drug effect"]],"tip":"Report with a printed, labeled strip and the patient's symptoms."}]},"cp":{"tab":"Community Care Meds","sub":"Home medication safety for community paramedics: HF, diabetes, anticoagulants, COPD and med reconciliation","intro":"Community paramedics see medicines where they are actually used: in the home. These tables cover the drug classes and warning signs most likely to drive readmissions and ER visits.","sections":[{"h":"Heart Failure Medicines at Home","p":"Guideline-directed therapy for HF with reduced ejection fraction usually combines four drug classes. Each has a home monitoring job.","cols":["Class / examples","What it does","Watch for and report"],"rows":[["ACE inhibitors (lisinopril, enalapril)","Lower afterload; slow heart remodeling","Dry cough, angioedema, high potassium, rising creatinine"],["ARBs (losartan, valsartan)","Block angiotensin II receptor; used when ACE cough occurs","Dizziness, high potassium, kidney changes"],["ARNI (sacubitril-valsartan)","Neprilysin inhibitor plus ARB; reduces HF deaths","Hypotension, angioedema; never overlapping with an ACE inhibitor"],["Beta-blockers (carvedilol, metoprolol succinate, bisoprolol)","Slow heart rate; improve survival over time","Bradycardia, fatigue, dizziness; rebound if abruptly missed"],["MRAs (spironolactone)","Aldosterone blockade; potassium-sparing","High potassium, breast tenderness or enlargement"],["SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin)","Mild diuresis; reduce HF hospitalization","Dehydration, genital infections, ketoacidosis"],["Digoxin","Raises contractility; slows AV node","Nausea, vision changes, slow or irregular pulse"]],"tip":"An ACE inhibitor and sacubitril-valsartan must never overlap: a washout of at least 36 hours is required."},{"h":"Diuretics, Daily Weights and Potassium","p":"Daily weights are the earliest home sign of fluid buildup, often days before shortness of breath.","cols":["Item","Key point"],"rows":[["Loop diuretics (furosemide, torsemide, bumetanide)","Strongest diuretics; can drop potassium and magnesium"],["Thiazides (hydrochlorothiazide, chlorthalidone, metolazone)","Lower potassium and sodium; metolazone strongly boosts loop effect"],["Potassium-sparing (spironolactone, triamterene)","Can raise potassium, especially with ACE inhibitors or ARBs"],["How to weigh","Same scale, each morning, after urinating, before eating, similar clothing"],["Typical report threshold","Gain of 2-3 lb in a day or about 5 lb in a week"],["Low potassium signs","Muscle cramps, weakness, palpitations, irregular pulse"],["High potassium signs","Weakness, slow pulse, peaked T waves on ECG"],["Hidden potassium sources","Salt substitutes (potassium chloride), supplements, some OTC products"],["Labs to ask about","Basic metabolic panel: potassium, sodium, creatinine after med changes"]],"tip":"Low potassium makes digoxin toxicity more likely even when the digoxin level is normal."},{"h":"Blood Pressure, Orthostasis and Falls","p":"Blood pressure medicines are a leading reversible cause of dizziness and falls in older adults.","cols":["Medicine / issue","Home concern","What to check or report"],"rows":[["Orthostatic hypotension","Drop of 20 systolic or 10 diastolic within 3 minutes of standing","Lying then standing vitals at 1 and 3 minutes"],["Diuretics","Volume depletion, especially in heat or illness","Dizziness, dry mouth, low urine output"],["ACE inhibitors / ARBs during vomiting or diarrhea","Hypotension and acute kidney injury","Report poor intake; ask about sick-day plan"],["Amlodipine, nifedipine","Ankle edema not from fluid overload","Edema without weight gain or lung crackles"],["Clonidine","Rebound hypertension if doses are missed","Headache, agitation, very high BP"],["Alpha-blockers (tamsulosin, doxazosin)","First-dose and positional dizziness","Falls after getting up at night"],["Beta-blockers","Slow pulse; can blunt low-sugar warning signs","Heart rate, fatigue, syncope"],["Nitrates plus PDE-5 inhibitors","Severe hypotension","Ask about sildenafil or tadalafil use"]],"tip":"Ask about falls at every visit: a new fall after a med change is a medication problem until proven otherwise."},{"h":"Insulin at Home","p":"Hypoglycemia timing follows the insulin's action profile.","cols":["Insulin","Onset / peak / duration","Hypoglycemia timing"],"rows":[["Rapid-acting (lispro, aspart, glulisine)","About 15 min / 1-2 h / 3-5 h","1-3 h after a mealtime injection, especially if the meal is missed"],["Regular (short-acting)","About 30 min / 2-4 h / 5-8 h","Mid-afternoon or late after meals"],["NPH (intermediate)","1-2 h / 4-12 h / 12-18 h","Overnight if given in the evening; mid-day if morning"],["Premixed 70/30","NPH plus regular profile","Peaks after meals and mid-day; needs regular meals"],["Glargine, detemir, degludec (basal)","Few hours / minimal peak / about 24 h or longer","Less peak-related lows; risk rises with poor intake"],["Low sugar symptoms","Shakiness, sweating, hunger, confusion","Nightmares or night sweats suggest nocturnal lows"],["Rule of 15","Fast-acting carbohydrate, recheck in about 15 minutes","Follow with a snack or meal once improved"],["Glucagon (injection or nasal)","For severe lows when unable to swallow safely","Less effective with depleted glycogen (alcohol, starvation)"]],"tip":"Open insulin pens and vials have limited room-temperature life; check the date on the device."},{"h":"Non-Insulin Diabetes Medicines","cols":["Class / examples","Home safety issue","Watch for and report"],"rows":[["Metformin","Lactic acidosis risk with kidney injury, dehydration, contrast","GI upset; long-term vitamin B12 deficiency"],["SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin, canagliflozin)","Euglycemic DKA with illness, fasting, surgery","Nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain with near-normal glucose"],["SGLT2 sick-day rules","Dehydration and ketosis risk during acute illness","Ask whether the patient has a written sick-day plan"],["GLP-1 agonists (semaglutide, dulaglutide, tirzepatide)","Nausea, vomiting, dehydration; slowed stomach emptying","Severe abdominal pain to the back (pancreatitis)"],["GLP-1 contraindication","Personal or family history of medullary thyroid cancer or MEN2","Neck mass or hoarseness"],["Sulfonylureas (glipizide, glimepiride, glyburide)","Hypoglycemia, prolonged in older adults and kidney disease","Lows when meals are skipped"],["DPP-4 inhibitors (sitagliptin, linagliptin)","Low hypoglycemia risk alone","Joint pain, rare pancreatitis"]],"tip":"Euglycemic DKA is easy to miss: a glucometer reading that looks fine does not rule out ketoacidosis on an SGLT2 inhibitor."},{"h":"Glucose Logs: What to Look For","cols":["Pattern","Possible meaning"],"rows":[["Repeated lows at the same time of day","Insulin or sulfonylurea peak outmatching food intake"],["High fasting readings after night sweats","Rebound after an overnight low; needs reporting"],["Rising readings during a steroid burst","Steroid-induced hyperglycemia, often afternoon and evening"],["Readings far higher than A1c suggests","Meter technique, expired strips, or unwashed hands"],["Big gaps or identical values","Missed testing or log copied from memory"],["Lows after weight loss on a GLP-1","Other glucose-lowering meds may need provider review"]]},{"h":"Anticoagulants at Home","p":"Anticoagulants, insulin and oral diabetes drugs are the top causes of ER visits for adverse drug events in older adults.","cols":["Drug","Key home teaching point","Monitoring / reversal"],"rows":[["Warfarin","Keep vitamin K intake consistent; many drug and antibiotic interactions","INR, usual target 2-3; vitamin K reverses"],["Apixaban","Twice daily; short half-life means missed doses lose protection fast","No routine lab; andexanet alfa reverses"],["Rivaroxaban","Higher strengths need food for absorption","No routine lab; andexanet alfa reverses"],["Dabigatran","Keep in original bottle; capsules not opened or crushed","No routine lab; idarucizumab reverses"],["Enoxaparin","Injection site bruising; kidney function matters","Anti-Xa level in special cases"],["Bleeding signs","Black stools, red or brown urine, gum or nose bleeding, large bruises","Report promptly"],["Head strike on anticoagulant","Delayed intracranial bleeding possible","Emergency evaluation even if alert"],["Added bleeding risk","NSAIDs, aspirin, clopidogrel, SSRIs, fish oil","Ask about OTC pain relievers"]],"tip":"Antibiotics like trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and metronidazole can sharply raise the INR."},{"h":"COPD Inhalers and Technique","p":"Poor technique is the most common reason an inhaler seems not to work.","cols":["Inhaler / topic","Role","Teaching point"],"rows":[["Albuterol, levalbuterol (SABA)","Rescue: relief in minutes","Rising rescue use signals worsening control"],["Albuterol-ipratropium","Rescue combination","Dry mouth; avoid spraying into eyes"],["Tiotropium, umeclidinium (LAMA)","Daily maintenance","Not for sudden symptoms"],["Salmeterol, formoterol, vilanterol (LABA)","Daily maintenance","Used with ICS or LAMA, not alone in asthma"],["Inhaled steroids (fluticasone, budesonide)","Reduce exacerbations","Rinse mouth and spit to prevent thrush"],["Metered-dose inhaler (MDI)","Slow deep breath at actuation","Shake if required, use spacer, keep breath in about 10 seconds"],["Dry-powder inhaler (DPI)","Fast, forceful breath in","Never exhale into the device; no spacer"],["Soft mist inhaler (Respimat)","Slow breath in","Prime as directed; slow deep inhalation"],["Steroid bursts (prednisone)","Short course for exacerbations","High glucose, insomnia, mood changes, fluid retention"]],"tip":"Count how many rescue inhalers were filled recently: frequent refills are an objective marker of poor control."},{"h":"Med Reconciliation and Adverse Drug Events","p":"A brown-bag review of every bottle in the home is the core community paramedic medication skill.","cols":["Issue","Example","What to do"],"rows":[["Duplication","Brand and generic of the same drug from two pharmacies","Report to prescriber and pharmacist"],["Hidden acetaminophen","Tylenol plus cold medicine or hydrocodone-acetaminophen","Count total sources; report"],["Beers: anticholinergics","Diphenhydramine, hydroxyzine, oxybutynin","Confusion, urinary retention, falls"],["Beers: sedatives","Benzodiazepines, zolpidem","Falls, fractures, delirium"],["Beers: long-acting sulfonylureas","Glyburide","Prolonged hypoglycemia"],["Beers: NSAIDs","Ibuprofen, naproxen in HF or kidney disease","Fluid retention, kidney injury, GI bleeding"],["Prescribing cascade","Amlodipine edema treated with a diuretic","Ask whether a new drug treats a side effect"],["Pill organizer exclusions","Sublingual nitroglycerin, dabigatran","Need original containers"],["Adherence barriers","Cost, confusion, vision, swallowing","Teach-back; refer to pharmacy or social work"],["Opioid plus sedative","Opioid with a benzodiazepine or gabapentinoid","Ask about naloxone in the home"]],"tip":"New confusion, falls or GI bleeding in an older adult: look at the med list first."}]},"tccc":{"tab":"TCCC & CBRN","sub":"Tactical combat casualty care and chemical, biological and radiological medicines","intro":"How medicines fit into tactical combat casualty care, from hemorrhage control through evacuation. It also covers antidotes and countermeasures for chemical, biological and radiological threats, following current CoTCCC guidelines conceptually.","sections":[{"h":"Phases of care and medications","p":"TCCC splits care into three phases. What is realistic to give depends on the tactical situation.","cols":["Phase","Focus","Medication role"],"rows":[["Care Under Fire","Return fire, move to cover, tourniquet for massive bleeding","No medications; treating the threat comes first"],["Tactical Field Care","MARCH PAWS assessment once out of direct fire","TXA, calcium, blood, analgesia, antibiotics begin here"],["TACEVAC","Casualty moved to higher care","Continue resuscitation, reassess pain, sedation, nausea"],["MARCH","Massive hemorrhage, Airway, Respiration, Circulation, Hypothermia/Head","Circulation step includes TXA and blood products"],["PAWS","Pain, Antibiotics, Wounds, Splinting","Analgesia and point-of-injury antibiotics"],["CBRN environment","Protect self, mask, escape, then treat","Antidote auto-injectors may be used in the hot zone"]],"tip":"Care Under Fire has no drug therapy; the tourniquet is the intervention."},{"h":"Hemorrhage and resuscitation","p":"Hemorrhage is the leading cause of preventable combat death. Medicines support clotting while bleeding is controlled.","cols":["Medicine or product","Role","Key point"],"rows":[["Tranexamic acid (TXA)","Antifibrinolytic; blocks plasminogen activation","Guidelines call for it as early as possible, within 3 hours"],["TXA late","Beyond 3 hours","Benefit lost and harm possible; injury time is documented"],["Calcium (chloride or gluconate)","Replaces calcium bound by citrate in blood products","Guidelines pair it with the first unit of blood product"],["Cold-stored low-titer O whole blood","Preferred resuscitation fluid","Replaces red cells, plasma and platelets together"],["Plasma and red cells (1:1)","Alternative when whole blood unavailable","Balanced component resuscitation"],["Crystalloid (LR, Plasma-Lyte)","Only if no blood products","Dilutes clotting factors; worsens coagulopathy"],["Hetastarch (Hextend)","Legacy colloid","Largely replaced by blood products"],["Hypertonic saline","Suspected TBI with herniation signs","Draws fluid from brain tissue"]],"tip":"Resuscitation endpoint: improved mental status and palpable radial pulse; higher pressure target with TBI."},{"h":"Hypothermia and the lethal triad","p":"Hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy feed each other. Hypocalcemia is often added as a fourth corner.","cols":["Element","Why it matters","Field prevention"],"rows":[["Hypothermia","Slows clotting enzymes and platelets","Minimize exposure, insulate from ground, active warming"],["Acidosis","Shock causes lactic acid buildup; impairs clotting","Bleeding control, perfusion restored with blood"],["Coagulopathy","Factor loss and dilution","Whole blood or balanced products, TXA"],["Hypocalcemia","Citrate in blood binds calcium","Calcium with blood products"],["Warmed fluids","Cold fluid lowers core temperature","In-line warmers for blood products"],["Hypothermia kits","Heat-reflective shell, active warming blanket","Used early, even in warm climates"]],"tip":"Every trauma casualty is at risk of hypothermia, even in the desert."},{"h":"Battlefield analgesia","p":"Pain options are matched to pain severity and whether the casualty is in shock or respiratory distress.","cols":["Option","Medicines","Watch for"],"rows":[["Mild to moderate pain, can fight","Combat wound medication pack: acetaminophen, meloxicam","Casualty stays alert and able to fight"],["Moderate to severe, no shock","Oral transmucosal fentanyl or ketamine","Sedation, breathing; secure the lozenge to a finger"],["Severe, in shock or respiratory distress","Ketamine","Preserves airway reflexes and blood pressure"],["Meloxicam","COX-2 preferential NSAID","Minimal platelet effect compared with older NSAIDs"],["Morphine (IV)","Legacy opioid option","Hypotension and respiratory depression"],["Naloxone","Opioid reversal","Reversal of analgesia; may need repeat"],["Ondansetron","Nausea and vomiting from opioids or ketamine","QT prolongation"],["Midazolam","Ketamine emergence reactions","Respiratory depression with opioids"]],"tip":"Older NSAIDs like ibuprofen are left out of the pack because they impair platelet function."},{"h":"Point-of-injury antibiotics","p":"Early antibiotics reduce infection in open combat wounds, including penetrating eye injury.","cols":["Route","Medicine","Notes"],"rows":[["Oral (can swallow)","Moxifloxacin","Broad fluoroquinolone in the medication pack"],["IV or IM (cannot take oral)","Ertapenem","Once-daily carbapenem"],["IV or IM (cannot take oral)","Ceftriaxone","Once-daily third-generation cephalosporin"],["Indication","All open combat wounds","Started in Tactical Field Care"],["Eye","Penetrating eye trauma","Rigid shield plus oral antibiotic"],["Allergy","Ask about drug allergies","Report and document"]],"tip":"Oral option if the casualty can swallow; IV or IM options if in shock or unable to take oral."},{"h":"Chemical agents","p":"Nerve agents and organophosphates block acetylcholinesterase, flooding synapses with acetylcholine.","cols":["Agent or sign","Treatment","Key point"],"rows":[["Muscarinic signs (SLUDGE/DUMBELS)","Atropine","Titrate to drying of secretions and easier breathing"],["Nicotinic signs (twitching, weakness)","Pralidoxime","Reactivates the enzyme before aging"],["Aging","Agent bond becomes permanent","Soman ages in minutes; oxime useless after"],["Seizures","Diazepam or midazolam","Midazolam auto-injector now available"],["Auto-injectors","Atropine-pralidoxime combination","Self-aid and buddy-aid kits"],["Cyanide","Hydroxocobalamin","Binds cyanide to form cyanocobalamin"],["Cyanide alternative","Sodium nitrite plus sodium thiosulfate","Nitrite causes methemoglobinemia"],["Anticholinergic agent (BZ)","Physostigmine","Reverses delirium; watch for bradycardia"]],"tip":"Pupil size and heart rate are poor atropine endpoints; secretions guide dosing."},{"h":"Decontamination","p":"Removing the agent stops ongoing exposure and protects providers.","cols":["Step","Purpose","Pearl"],"rows":[["Mask and protect self","Avoid becoming a casualty","Self-aid with auto-injectors comes first if symptomatic"],["Remove clothing","Removes most contamination","Cut away, roll outward"],["Dry decon","Blot liquid agent","Absorbent material or RSDL lotion"],["Wet decon","Soap and water flush","Gentle washing; skin damage increases absorption"],["Lifesaving care first","Hemorrhage and airway before full decon","Radiation contamination never delays critical care"],["Off-gassing","Vapor trapped in clothing","Ventilate and contain removed clothing"]]},{"h":"Biological threats","p":"Many bioterror agents are bacteria treated or prevented with common oral antibiotics.","cols":["Threat","Medicine","Notes"],"rows":[["Inhalational anthrax exposure","Ciprofloxacin or doxycycline","Prophylaxis course is about 60 days with vaccine"],["Anthrax disease","IV combination antibiotics plus antitoxin","Widened mediastinum on chest film"],["Plague","Doxycycline or ciprofloxacin","Pneumonic plague spreads person to person"],["Tularemia","Doxycycline or ciprofloxacin","Not spread person to person"],["Doxycycline","Tetracycline class","Photosensitivity, esophageal irritation"],["Ciprofloxacin","Fluoroquinolone","Tendon problems, QT prolongation"]]},{"h":"Radiological countermeasures","p":"Each countermeasure targets a specific isotope; none protects against all radiation.","cols":["Medicine","Target","Key point"],"rows":[["Potassium iodide","Radioactive iodine only","Protects thyroid only; best near time of exposure"],["Prussian blue","Cesium and thallium","Binds in gut; blue stool, constipation"],["Ca-DTPA","Plutonium, americium, curium","Preferred in first 24 hours"],["Zn-DTPA","Same transuranic elements","Maintenance; preferred in pregnancy"],["Ondansetron","Radiation-induced vomiting","Time to vomiting helps estimate dose"],["Filgrastim-type agents","Bone marrow suppression","Hematopoietic syndrome of acute radiation sickness"]],"tip":"Potassium iodide does nothing for external radiation or non-iodine isotopes."}]}};
